window.BAHR_DATA=window.BAHR_DATA||{};window.BAHR_DATA["passages-094"]=[{"id":"K094.01","q":"94:1–4","s":94,"surah":"al-Sharḥ","surah_ar":"الشرح","title":"al-Sharḥ · Heading and verses 1–4","cairo":"K IV:770–772 · T XVI:492–503","source_page_first":770,"source_page_last":772,"chunk":"passages-094","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Heading and verses 1–4","html":"<p>Meccan; eight verses. [K-DKA adds: revealed after al-Ḍuḥā.]\nIn the name of God, the All-Merciful, the Merciful.\n“Have We not expanded your breast for you, and lifted from you your burden, which made your back creak, and raised your renown for you?”</p>","author":"kashshaf","source_id":"K094.01"}],"types":["exposition","language","theology","reports"],"ar":"مكية، وآياتها 8 «نزلت بعد الضحى» بسم الله الرحمن الرحيم\nبسم الله الرحمن الرحيم\nألم نشرح لك صدرك (1) ووضعنا عنك وزرك (2) الذي أنقض ظهرك (3) ورفعنا لك ذكرك (4)","layers":[{"id":"K094.01","heading":"K094.01 — Heading and verses 1–4","text":"Meccan; eight verses. [K-DKA adds: revealed after al-Ḍuḥā.]\nIn the name of God, the All-Merciful, the Merciful.\n“Have We not expanded your breast for you, and lifted from you your burden, which made your back creak, and raised your renown for you?”","title":"Heading and verses 1–4","author":"kashshaf","surah":94,"ar":"مكية، وآياتها 8 «نزلت بعد الضحى» بسم الله الرحمن الرحيم\n\n\n\nبسم الله الرحمن الرحيم\n\nألم نشرح لك صدرك (1) ووضعنا عنك وزرك (2) الذي أنقض ظهرك (3) ورفعنا لك ذكرك (4)","html":"<p>Meccan; eight verses. [K-DKA adds: revealed after al-Ḍuḥā.]\nIn the name of God, the All-Merciful, the Merciful.\n“Have We not expanded your breast for you, and lifted from you your burden, which made your back creak, and raised your renown for you?”</p>","lemma":""}],"cairo_notes":[],"verses":[{"key":"94:1","number":1,"ar":"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَـٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ أَلَمْ نَشْرَحْ لَكَ صَدْرَكَ","en":"Have We not caused thy bosom to dilate"},{"key":"94:2","number":2,"ar":"وَوَضَعْنَا عَنكَ وِزْرَكَ","en":"And eased thee of the burden"},{"key":"94:3","number":3,"ar":"ٱلَّذِىٓ أَنقَضَ ظَهْرَكَ","en":"Which weighed down thy back"},{"key":"94:4","number":4,"ar":"وَرَفَعْنَا لَكَ ذِكْرَكَ","en":"And exalted thy fame"}],"quran_ar":"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَـٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ أَلَمْ نَشْرَحْ لَكَ صَدْرَكَ ﴿1﴾ وَوَضَعْنَا عَنكَ وِزْرَكَ ﴿2﴾ ٱلَّذِىٓ أَنقَضَ ظَهْرَكَ ﴿3﴾ وَرَفَعْنَا لَكَ ذِكْرَكَ ﴿4﴾","quran_en":"1. Have We not caused thy bosom to dilate 2. And eased thee of the burden 3. Which weighed down thy back 4. And exalted thy fame"},{"id":"K094.02","q":"94:1–4","s":94,"surah":"al-Sharḥ","surah_ar":"الشرح","title":"al-Sharḥ · Expansion and the burden","cairo":"K IV:770–772 · T XVI:492–503","source_page_first":770,"source_page_last":772,"chunk":"passages-094","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Expansion and the burden","html":"<p>The question repudiates the absence of expansion, and thus affirms and establishes that the expansion occurred. It is as though He had said, “We expanded your breast for you.” Accordingly, “and We lifted” is coordinated with it in consideration of its meaning.\n“We expanded your breast” means: We made it spacious enough to accommodate the concerns of prophethood and the calling of both classes of accountable beings, humans and jinn, together; or spacious enough to bear the hardships with which the unbelievers of your people and others confront you; or We expanded it by the knowledge and wisdom We deposited in it, removing the constriction and distress accompanying blindness and ignorance. Al-Ḥasan said: “It was filled with wisdom and knowledge.”\nAbū Jaʿfar al-Manṣūr is reported to have read a-lam nashraḥa <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(نَشْرَحَ)</bdi> laka <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(لك)</bdi>, giving the ḥāʾ <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(حاء (ح))</bdi> a fatḥa <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(فَتْحَة (ـَ))</bdi>. They said: perhaps he articulated the ḥāʾ <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(حاء (ح))</bdi> distinctly and fully at its point of articulation, so that the listener thought he had pronounced a fatḥa <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(فَتْحَة (ـَ))</bdi>.\nThe burden that made his back creak—that is, caused it to emit naqīḍ <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(نقيض)</bdi>, the sound of creaking and coming apart because of its weight—is a figure for what weighed upon God&#x27;s Messenger and distressed him: lapses before prophethood; or his lack of knowledge of rulings and revealed laws; or his desperate eagerness for the obstinate among his people to accept Islam and his grief over them. Lifting it from him means forgiving him; or teaching him the revealed laws; or establishing his excuse after he had conveyed the message and exerted himself fully in conveying it.\nAnas read “and We loosened” (wa-ḥalalnā <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(وحللنا)</bdi>), and “and We laid down” (wa-ḥaṭaṭnā <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(وحططنا)</bdi>). Ibn Masʿūd read: “and We loosened from you your heavy load” (wa-ḥalalnā <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(وحللنا)</bdi> ʿanka <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(عنك)</bdi> wiqraka).</p>","author":"kashshaf","source_id":"K094.02"},{"type":"exposition","title":"The force of the question (XVI, 492)","html":"<p>On “it affirms and establishes the expansion”: He repudiates the absence of expansion. Once that absence is repudiated, expansion is established, because the interrogative hamza <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(همزة (ء))</bdi> expresses repudiation; repudiation is negation, and negation applied to negation becomes affirmation. The hamza <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(همزة (ء))</bdi> may not be treated here as a question seeking acknowledgment (taqrīr <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(تقرير)</bdi>).</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T094.01"},{"type":"exposition","title":"The breadth of the Prophet's breast (492–493)","html":"<p>On “We made it spacious enough to accommodate the concerns of prophethood and the calling of both classes of beings”: if you ask why he explains expansion of the breast here more comprehensively and expansively than at “He said: My Lord, expand my breast for me” (20:25), where he says, “When he was commanded to go to Pharaoh the tyrant, he knew that he had been charged with a mighty undertaking and a tremendous affair, requiring endurance possible only for someone with a steadfast heart and an expansive breast; he therefore asked his Lord to grant him expansion of his breast”—I answer: concerns are proportionate to aspirations. How well al-Ṣāḥib said:\n“A woman said: Why have anxieties assailed you,\nwhen your command is obeyed among the nations?\nI said: Leave me with the grief that chokes me;\nanxieties are proportionate to aspirations.”\nEvery situation calls for its own discourse. When the one to whom God spoke was sent to Pharaoh the tyrant, he requested expansion, as God says: “Go to Pharaoh: he has exceeded all bounds. He said: My Lord, expand my breast for me” (20:24–25). But when the Beloved was summoned to the station of “two bow-lengths, or nearer” (53:9), he was told, “Have We not expanded your breast for you?” This will appear in the report of Mālik <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(مَالِك)</bdi> ibn Ṣaʿṣaʿa.\nJaʿfar al-Ṣādiq said: “Have We not expanded your breast for you to witness Me and behold Me?” Ibn ʿAṭāʾ said: “Have We not emptied your innermost consciousness of everything, so that you ceased to behold created existence and whatever is other than the Real? Your breast was expanded for vision, whereas Moses&#x27; breast was expanded for speech.” Sahl said: “Have We not broadened your breast with the light of messengership, making it a repository of realities?”</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T094.02"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Opening the breast; the root meaning of sharḥ (493–494)","html":"<p>On al-Ḥasan&#x27;s saying, “It was filled with wisdom and knowledge”: perhaps he alludes to what we have transmitted from al-Bukhārī, Muslim, al-Tirmidhī, and al-Nasāʾī, from Mālik <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(مَالِك)</bdi> ibn Ṣaʿṣaʿa, from the Prophet: “While I was beside the House, between sleeping and waking, a basin of gold containing Zamzam water was brought to me, and my breast was opened as far as such-and-such.” Qatāda said—addressing Anas—“What does he mean?” He replied: “To the bottom of my belly.” He continued: “My heart was taken out and washed with Zamzam water, then put back in its place, then filled with faith and wisdom. Then an animal smaller than a mule and larger than a donkey was brought to me”—the lengthy report continues.\nThe Imām [Fakhr al-Dīn al-Rāzī] said: “It is not unlikely that the black blood they washed from his heart was a sign of inclination and readiness to commit sins, and reluctance to perform acts of obedience. Once they removed it, that was a sign that its possessor would persevere in obedience and guard against evil deeds. God does whatever He wills and decrees whatever He wishes.”\nAl-Rāghib said: “The original sense of sharḥ <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(شرح)</bdi> is spreading out flesh and the like. One says sharaḥtu <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(شرحت)</bdi> al-laḥma <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(اللحمة)</bdi> and sharraḥtuhu <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(شرّحته)</bdi>. From this comes expansion of the breast: its spreading out through divine light, tranquility from God, and a spirit from Him.”</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T094.03"},{"type":"exposition","title":"The reading nashraḥa (494–495)","html":"<p>On “he read a-lam nashraḥa <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(نَشْرَحَ)</bdi>, with fatḥa <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(فَتْحَة (ـَ))</bdi> on the ḥāʾ <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(حاء (ح))</bdi>”: its original form is nashraḥan <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(نَشْرَحَنْ)</bdi>. The nūn <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(نون (ن))</bdi> was omitted and the fatḥa <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(فَتْحَة (ـَ))</bdi> on the ḥāʾ <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(حاء (ح))</bdi> retained as an indication of it. This is in al-Muntaqā.\nIbn Jinnī said: “A-lam nashraḥa <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(نَشْرَحَ)</bdi>, with fatḥa <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(فَتْحَة (ـَ))</bdi> on the ḥāʾ <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(حاء (ح))</bdi>, is transmitted from Abū Jaʿfar al-Manṣūr. Ibn Mujāhid said: ‘This is wholly impermissible.’” Ibn Jinnī said: “The obvious position, and what accords with customary usage, is as Ibn Mujāhid states. Something like it nevertheless occurs in what I read to Abū ʿAlī in Abū Zayd&#x27;s Nawādir:\n‘From which of death&#x27;s two days shall I flee—\na day not decreed, or a day decreed?’\nIt is said that he intended lam <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(لم)</bdi> yuqdar-an, with the light emphatic nūn <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(نون (ن))</bdi>. In our view omitting it is impermissible, because the emphatic nūn <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(نون (ن))</bdi> is most closely allied to expansion and elaboration, not concision and abbreviation. In Abū Zayd&#x27;s Nawādir there is another verse, said to be fabricated:\n‘Strike away the anxieties that visit you by night,\nas with a sword you strike a horse&#x27;s forelock.’\nHe intended iḍrib-an <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(اضربَنْ)</bdi>, with the light nūn <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(نون (ن))</bdi>, and omitted it.”</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T094.04"},{"type":"exposition","title":"The burden and the ordered correspondence of its meanings (495)","html":"<p>On “the sound of creaking and coming apart”: al-Ṣiḥāḥ says: “Anqaḍa al-ḥimlu ẓahrahu <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(أنقض الحمل ظهره)</bdi> means that the load weighed upon his back. Its original sense concerns sound. Naqīḍ <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(نقيض)</bdi> is the sound of camel-litters and riding gear.” Al-Rāghib says: “‘It made his back creak’ means that it broke it until it produced a creaking sound; the naqīḍ <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(نقيض)</bdi> of joints is their sound. ‘Back’ is a metaphor, likening sins to a load that weighs its bearer down.”\nOn “lifting it from him means forgiving him”: the expression consists of a subject and predicate. This sentence is coordinated with a corresponding sentence, “the burden is a figure”—that is, a metaphor preceded by a comparison. “And We lifted” is then a reinforcement of the metaphor (tarshīḥ <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(ترشيح)</bdi>), because it is a description appropriate to that from which the metaphor is drawn. This is what he means by “lifting it from him means forgiving him,” and the rest.\nWhen “burden” is borrowed for sin, its reinforcement is appropriately understood as forgiveness. When it is borrowed for ignorance of rulings, the fitting explanation is instruction in the revealed laws. When it is understood as his desperate eagerness for their acceptance of Islam, the corresponding interpretation is the establishment of his excuse: do not be consumed by eagerness for their guidance or let yourself perish in grief over them, for you have exerted yourself in conveying the message and have established the proof against them. This is the rhetorical pattern of gathering and ordered distribution (laff wa-nashr).</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T094.05"}],"types":["exposition","language","theology","reports"],"ar":"استفهم عن انتفاء الشرح على وجه الإنكار، فأفاد إثبات الشرح وإيجابه، فكأنه قيل:\nشرحنا لك صدرك، ولذلك عطف عليه: وضعنا: اعتبارا للمعنى. ومعنى: شرحنا صدرك:\nفسحناه حتى وسع عموم النبوة ودعوة الثقلين جميعا. أو حتى احتمل المكاره التي يتعرض لك بها كفار قومك وغيرهم: أو فسحناه بما أودعناه من العلوم والحكم، وأزلنا عنه الضيق والحرج الذي يكون مع العمى والجهل. وعن الحسن: مليء حكمة وعلما. وعن أبى جعفر المنصور أنه قرأ: ألم نشرح لك، بفتح الحاء. وقالوا: لعله بين الحاء وأشبعها في مخرجها، فظن السامع أنه فتحها، والوزر الذي أنقض ظهره- أى حمله على النقيض وهو صوت الانتقاض والانفكاك لثقله- مثل لما كان يثقل على رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم ويغمه من فرطاته قبل النبوة. أو من جهله بالأحكام والشرائع. أو من تهالكه على إسلام أولى العناد من قومه وتلهفه. ووضعه عنه: أن غفر له، أو علم الشرائع، أو مهد عذره بعد ما بلغ وبلغ. وقرأ أنس:\nوحللنا، وحططنا. وقرأ ابن مسعود: وحللنا عنك وقرك.\nسورة {ألم نشرح}\nمكية، وهي ثماني آيات\nبسم الله الرحمن الرحيم\nقوله: (فأفاد إثبات الشرح وإيجابه)، أي: أنكر عدم الشرح، فإذا أنكر ذلك ثبت الشرح، لأن الهمزة للإنكار، والإنكار نفي، والنفي إذا دخل على النفي عاد إثباتا، ولا يجوز جعل الهزة للتقرير.\nقوله: (فسحناه حتى وسع هموم النبوة ودعوة الثقلين جميعا)، فإن قلت: لم فسر هاهنا شرح الصدر أجمع وأشرح من تفسيره في قوله تعالى: {قال رب اشرح لي صدري} [طه: 25]، حيث قال: \"لما أمره بالذهاب إلى فرعون الطاغي، عرف أنه كلف أمرا عظيما وخطبا جسيما،\nيحتاج معه إلى احتمال ما لا يحتمله إلا ذو جأش رابط وصدر فسيح، فاستوهب ربه أن يشرح صدره؟ \". قلت: إن الهموم بقدر الهمم، ونعم ما قال الصاحب:\nوقائلة لم عزتك الهموم وأمرك ممتثل في الأمم؟\nفقلت: ذريني على غصتي فإن الهموم بقدر الهمم\nولكل مقام مقال؛ فإن الكليم حين بعث إلى فرعون الطاغي، طلب الانشراح كما قال: {اذهب إلى فرعون إنه طغى (قال رب اشرح لي صدري} [طه: 24 - 25]، والحبيب لما طلب إلى مقام {قاب قوسين أو أدنى} [النجم: 9]، قيل له: {ألم نشرح لك صدرك}، كما يجيء في حديث مالك بن صعصعة.\nوقال جعفر الصادق: \"ألم نشرح لك صدرك لمشاهدتي ومطالعتي. وقال ابن عطاء: ألم نخل سرك عن الكل، فغبت عن مشاهدة الكون وما سوى الحق، فشرح صدرك للنظر، وشرح صدر موسى للكلام. وقال سهل: ألم نوسع صدرك بنور الرسالة، فجعلناه معدنا للحقائق\".\nقوله: (وعن الحسن: ملئ حكمة وعلما)، لعله يشير إلى ما رويناه عن البخاري ومسلم والترمذي والنسائي، عن مالك بن صعصعة، عن النبي? : \"بينا أنا عند البيت بين النائم واليقظان، فأتيت بطست من ذهب فيها ماء زمزم، فشرح صدري إلى كذا وكذا. قال قتادة: يعني لأنس: ما يعني؟ قال: إلى أسفل بطني، قال: فاستخرج قلبي فغسل بماء زمزم، ثم أعيد مكانه، ثم حشي إيمانا وحكمة، ثم أتي بدابة دون البغل وفوق الحمار\" الحديث بطوله.\nقال الإمام: \"لا يبعد أن يكون حصول الدم الأسود الذي غسلوه من قلبه صلوات الله عليه، علامة الميل والركون إلى المعاصي والتحجم عن الطاعات، فإذا أزالوه عنه كان ذلك علامة كون صاحبة مواظبا على الطاعات محترزا عن السيئات، يفعل الله ما يشاء ويحكم ما يريد\". الراغب: \" أصل الشرح بسط اللحم ونحوه، يقال: شرحت اللحم وشرحته، ومنه شرح الصدر، وهو بسطه بنور إلهي وسكينة من جهة الله وروح منه\".\nقوله: (قرأ: \"ألم نشرح\" بفتح الحاء)، أصله: \"نشرحن\"، فحذف وأبقى فتحة الحاء دليلا على النون في \"المنتقى\"، قال ابن جنى: \"رويت عن أبي جعفر المنصور: \"ألم نشرح\"، بفتح الحاء، قال ابن مجاهد: \"هذا غير جائز أصلا\". وقال ابن جني: \"ظاهر الأمر ومألوف الاستعمال ما ذكره ابن مجاهد، لكن جاء مثل هذا فيما قرأت على أبي علي في نوادر أبي زيد:\nمن أي يومي من الموت أفر أيوم لم يقدر أم يوم قدر؟\nقيل: أراد: لم يقدرن، بالنون الخفيفة، وحذفها عندنا غير جائز، لأن نون التأكيد أشبه شيء به الإسهاب والإطناب، لا الإيجاز والاختصار. وفي نوادر أبي زيد أيضا بيت آخر، ويقال إنه مصنوع، وهو قوله:\nاضرب عنك الهموم طارقها ضربك بالسيف قونس الفرس\nأراد: اضربن، بالنون الخفيفة، وحذفها\".\nقوله: (وهو صوت الانتقاض والانفكاك)، وفي \"الصحاح\": \"أنقض الحمل ظهره، أي: أثقله. وأصله الصوت، والنقيض: صوت المحامل والرحال\".\nالراغب: \"أنقض ظهره: أي: كسره حتى صار له نقيض، ونقيض المفاصل صوتها. والظهر استعارة تشبيها للذنوب بالحمل الذي ينوء بحامله\".\nقوله: (ووضعه عنه: أن غفر له)، مبتدأ وخبر، والجملة معطوفة على مثلها وهي قوله: \"والوزر مثل\"، أي: استعارة مسبوقة بالتشبيه، فيكون {ووضعنا} ترشيحا لها، لأنه وصف مناسب للمستعار منه. هذا هو المعنى بقوله: \"ووضعه عنه: أن غفر له\" إلى آخره؛ فإذا استعير الوزر للذنب، فالمناسب أن يحمل الترشيح على معنى الغفران، وإذا استعير للجهل بالأحكام، فالملائم أن يجري على تعليم الشرائع، وإذا حمل على تهالكه صلوات الله عليه على إسلامهم، فالموافق أن يتأول بتمهيد العذر، أي: لا تحرص على هداهم، ولا تذهب نفسك عليهم حسرات، لأنك بالغت في التبليغ، وألزمت عليهم الحجة، ففيه لف ونشر.","layers":[{"id":"K094.02","heading":"K094.02 — Expansion and the burden","text":"The question repudiates the absence of expansion, and thus affirms and establishes that the expansion occurred. It is as though He had said, “We expanded your breast for you.” Accordingly, “and We lifted” is coordinated with it in consideration of its meaning.\n“We expanded your breast” means: We made it spacious enough to accommodate the concerns of prophethood and the calling of both classes of accountable beings, humans and jinn, together; or spacious enough to bear the hardships with which the unbelievers of your people and others confront you; or We expanded it by the knowledge and wisdom We deposited in it, removing the constriction and distress accompanying blindness and ignorance. Al-Ḥasan said: “It was filled with wisdom and knowledge.”\nAbū Jaʿfar al-Manṣūr is reported to have read a-lam nashraḥa laka, giving the ḥāʾ a fatḥa. They said: perhaps he articulated the ḥāʾ distinctly and fully at its point of articulation, so that the listener thought he had pronounced a fatḥa.\nThe burden that made his back creak—that is, caused it to emit naqīḍ, the sound of creaking and coming apart because of its weight—is a figure for what weighed upon God's Messenger and distressed him: lapses before prophethood; or his lack of knowledge of rulings and revealed laws; or his desperate eagerness for the obstinate among his people to accept Islam and his grief over them. Lifting it from him means forgiving him; or teaching him the revealed laws; or establishing his excuse after he had conveyed the message and exerted himself fully in conveying it.\nAnas read “and We loosened” (wa-ḥalalnā), and “and We laid down” (wa-ḥaṭaṭnā). Ibn Masʿūd read: “and We loosened from you your heavy load” (wa-ḥalalnā ʿanka wiqraka).","title":"Expansion and the burden","author":"kashshaf","surah":94,"ar":"استفهم عن انتفاء الشرح على وجه الإنكار، فأفاد إثبات الشرح وإيجابه، فكأنه قيل:\n\nشرحنا لك صدرك، ولذلك عطف عليه: وضعنا: اعتبارا للمعنى. ومعنى: شرحنا صدرك:\n\nفسحناه حتى وسع عموم النبوة ودعوة الثقلين جميعا. أو حتى احتمل المكاره التي يتعرض لك بها كفار قومك وغيرهم: أو فسحناه بما أودعناه من العلوم والحكم، وأزلنا عنه الضيق والحرج الذي يكون مع العمى والجهل. وعن الحسن: مليء حكمة وعلما. وعن أبى جعفر المنصور أنه قرأ: ألم نشرح لك، بفتح الحاء. وقالوا: لعله بين الحاء وأشبعها في مخرجها، فظن السامع أنه فتحها، والوزر الذي أنقض ظهره- أى حمله على النقيض وهو صوت الانتقاض والانفكاك لثقله- مثل لما كان يثقل على رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم ويغمه من فرطاته قبل النبوة. أو من جهله بالأحكام والشرائع. أو من تهالكه على إسلام أولى العناد من قومه وتلهفه. ووضعه عنه: أن غفر له، أو علم الشرائع، أو مهد عذره بعد ما بلغ وبلغ. وقرأ أنس:\n\nوحللنا، وحططنا. وقرأ ابن مسعود: وحللنا عنك وقرك.","html":"<p>The question repudiates the absence of expansion, and thus affirms and establishes that the expansion occurred. It is as though He had said, “We expanded your breast for you.” Accordingly, “and We lifted” is coordinated with it in consideration of its meaning.\n“We expanded your breast” means: We made it spacious enough to accommodate the concerns of prophethood and the calling of both classes of accountable beings, humans and jinn, together; or spacious enough to bear the hardships with which the unbelievers of your people and others confront you; or We expanded it by the knowledge and wisdom We deposited in it, removing the constriction and distress accompanying blindness and ignorance. Al-Ḥasan said: “It was filled with wisdom and knowledge.”\nAbū Jaʿfar al-Manṣūr is reported to have read a-lam nashraḥa <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(نَشْرَحَ)</bdi> laka <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(لك)</bdi>, giving the ḥāʾ <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(حاء (ح))</bdi> a fatḥa <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(فَتْحَة (ـَ))</bdi>. They said: perhaps he articulated the ḥāʾ <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(حاء (ح))</bdi> distinctly and fully at its point of articulation, so that the listener thought he had pronounced a fatḥa <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(فَتْحَة (ـَ))</bdi>.\nThe burden that made his back creak—that is, caused it to emit naqīḍ <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(نقيض)</bdi>, the sound of creaking and coming apart because of its weight—is a figure for what weighed upon God&#x27;s Messenger and distressed him: lapses before prophethood; or his lack of knowledge of rulings and revealed laws; or his desperate eagerness for the obstinate among his people to accept Islam and his grief over them. Lifting it from him means forgiving him; or teaching him the revealed laws; or establishing his excuse after he had conveyed the message and exerted himself fully in conveying it.\nAnas read “and We loosened” (wa-ḥalalnā <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(وحللنا)</bdi>), and “and We laid down” (wa-ḥaṭaṭnā <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(وحططنا)</bdi>). Ibn Masʿūd read: “and We loosened from you your heavy load” (wa-ḥalalnā <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(وحللنا)</bdi> ʿanka <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(عنك)</bdi> wiqraka).</p>","lemma":""},{"id":"T094.01","heading":"T094.01 — The force of the question (XVI, 492)","text":"On “it affirms and establishes the expansion”: He repudiates the absence of expansion. Once that absence is repudiated, expansion is established, because the interrogative hamza expresses repudiation; repudiation is negation, and negation applied to negation becomes affirmation. The hamza may not be treated here as a question seeking acknowledgment (taqrīr).","title":"The force of the question (XVI, 492)","author":"tibi","on":["K094.02"],"anchor":"K094.02","surah":94,"ar":"سورة {ألم نشرح}\n\nمكية، وهي ثماني آيات\n\nبسم الله الرحمن الرحيم\n\nقوله: (فأفاد إثبات الشرح وإيجابه)، أي: أنكر عدم الشرح، فإذا أنكر ذلك ثبت الشرح، لأن الهمزة للإنكار، والإنكار نفي، والنفي إذا دخل على النفي عاد إثباتا، ولا يجوز جعل الهزة للتقرير.","html":"<p>On “it affirms and establishes the expansion”: He repudiates the absence of expansion. Once that absence is repudiated, expansion is established, because the interrogative hamza <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(همزة (ء))</bdi> expresses repudiation; repudiation is negation, and negation applied to negation becomes affirmation. The hamza <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(همزة (ء))</bdi> may not be treated here as a question seeking acknowledgment (taqrīr <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(تقرير)</bdi>).</p>","lemma":"فأفاد إثبات الشرح وإيجابه"},{"id":"T094.02","heading":"T094.02 — The breadth of the Prophet's breast (492–493)","text":"On “We made it spacious enough to accommodate the concerns of prophethood and the calling of both classes of beings”: if you ask why he explains expansion of the breast here more comprehensively and expansively than at “He said: My Lord, expand my breast for me” (20:25), where he says, “When he was commanded to go to Pharaoh the tyrant, he knew that he had been charged with a mighty undertaking and a tremendous affair, requiring endurance possible only for someone with a steadfast heart and an expansive breast; he therefore asked his Lord to grant him expansion of his breast”—I answer: concerns are proportionate to aspirations. How well al-Ṣāḥib said:\n“A woman said: Why have anxieties assailed you,\nwhen your command is obeyed among the nations?\nI said: Leave me with the grief that chokes me;\nanxieties are proportionate to aspirations.”\nEvery situation calls for its own discourse. When the one to whom God spoke was sent to Pharaoh the tyrant, he requested expansion, as God says: “Go to Pharaoh: he has exceeded all bounds. He said: My Lord, expand my breast for me” (20:24–25). But when the Beloved was summoned to the station of “two bow-lengths, or nearer” (53:9), he was told, “Have We not expanded your breast for you?” This will appear in the report of Mālik ibn Ṣaʿṣaʿa.\nJaʿfar al-Ṣādiq said: “Have We not expanded your breast for you to witness Me and behold Me?” Ibn ʿAṭāʾ said: “Have We not emptied your innermost consciousness of everything, so that you ceased to behold created existence and whatever is other than the Real? Your breast was expanded for vision, whereas Moses' breast was expanded for speech.” Sahl said: “Have We not broadened your breast with the light of messengership, making it a repository of realities?”","title":"The breadth of the Prophet's breast (492–493)","author":"tibi","on":["K094.02"],"anchor":"K094.02","surah":94,"ar":"قوله: (فسحناه حتى وسع هموم النبوة ودعوة الثقلين جميعا)، فإن قلت: لم فسر هاهنا شرح الصدر أجمع وأشرح من تفسيره في قوله تعالى: {قال رب اشرح لي صدري} [طه: 25]، حيث قال: \"لما أمره بالذهاب إلى فرعون الطاغي، عرف أنه كلف أمرا عظيما وخطبا جسيما،\n\nيحتاج معه إلى احتمال ما لا يحتمله إلا ذو جأش رابط وصدر فسيح، فاستوهب ربه أن يشرح صدره؟ \". قلت: إن الهموم بقدر الهمم، ونعم ما قال الصاحب:\n\nوقائلة لم عزتك الهموم وأمرك ممتثل في الأمم؟\n\nفقلت: ذريني على غصتي فإن الهموم بقدر الهمم\n\nولكل مقام مقال؛ فإن الكليم حين بعث إلى فرعون الطاغي، طلب الانشراح كما قال: {اذهب إلى فرعون إنه طغى (قال رب اشرح لي صدري} [طه: 24 - 25]، والحبيب لما طلب إلى مقام {قاب قوسين أو أدنى} [النجم: 9]، قيل له: {ألم نشرح لك صدرك}، كما يجيء في حديث مالك بن صعصعة.\n\nوقال جعفر الصادق: \"ألم نشرح لك صدرك لمشاهدتي ومطالعتي. وقال ابن عطاء: ألم نخل سرك عن الكل، فغبت عن مشاهدة الكون وما سوى الحق، فشرح صدرك للنظر، وشرح صدر موسى للكلام. وقال سهل: ألم نوسع صدرك بنور الرسالة، فجعلناه معدنا للحقائق\".","html":"<p>On “We made it spacious enough to accommodate the concerns of prophethood and the calling of both classes of beings”: if you ask why he explains expansion of the breast here more comprehensively and expansively than at “He said: My Lord, expand my breast for me” (20:25), where he says, “When he was commanded to go to Pharaoh the tyrant, he knew that he had been charged with a mighty undertaking and a tremendous affair, requiring endurance possible only for someone with a steadfast heart and an expansive breast; he therefore asked his Lord to grant him expansion of his breast”—I answer: concerns are proportionate to aspirations. How well al-Ṣāḥib said:\n“A woman said: Why have anxieties assailed you,\nwhen your command is obeyed among the nations?\nI said: Leave me with the grief that chokes me;\nanxieties are proportionate to aspirations.”\nEvery situation calls for its own discourse. When the one to whom God spoke was sent to Pharaoh the tyrant, he requested expansion, as God says: “Go to Pharaoh: he has exceeded all bounds. He said: My Lord, expand my breast for me” (20:24–25). But when the Beloved was summoned to the station of “two bow-lengths, or nearer” (53:9), he was told, “Have We not expanded your breast for you?” This will appear in the report of Mālik <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(مَالِك)</bdi> ibn Ṣaʿṣaʿa.\nJaʿfar al-Ṣādiq said: “Have We not expanded your breast for you to witness Me and behold Me?” Ibn ʿAṭāʾ said: “Have We not emptied your innermost consciousness of everything, so that you ceased to behold created existence and whatever is other than the Real? Your breast was expanded for vision, whereas Moses&#x27; breast was expanded for speech.” Sahl said: “Have We not broadened your breast with the light of messengership, making it a repository of realities?”</p>","lemma":"فسحناه حتى وسع هموم النبوة ودعوة الثقلين جميعا"},{"id":"T094.03","heading":"T094.03 — Opening the breast; the root meaning of sharḥ (493–494)","text":"On al-Ḥasan's saying, “It was filled with wisdom and knowledge”: perhaps he alludes to what we have transmitted from al-Bukhārī, Muslim, al-Tirmidhī, and al-Nasāʾī, from Mālik ibn Ṣaʿṣaʿa, from the Prophet: “While I was beside the House, between sleeping and waking, a basin of gold containing Zamzam water was brought to me, and my breast was opened as far as such-and-such.” Qatāda said—addressing Anas—“What does he mean?” He replied: “To the bottom of my belly.” He continued: “My heart was taken out and washed with Zamzam water, then put back in its place, then filled with faith and wisdom. Then an animal smaller than a mule and larger than a donkey was brought to me”—the lengthy report continues.\nThe Imām [Fakhr al-Dīn al-Rāzī] said: “It is not unlikely that the black blood they washed from his heart was a sign of inclination and readiness to commit sins, and reluctance to perform acts of obedience. Once they removed it, that was a sign that its possessor would persevere in obedience and guard against evil deeds. God does whatever He wills and decrees whatever He wishes.”\nAl-Rāghib said: “The original sense of sharḥ is spreading out flesh and the like. One says sharaḥtu al-laḥma and sharraḥtuhu. From this comes expansion of the breast: its spreading out through divine light, tranquility from God, and a spirit from Him.”","title":"Opening the breast; the root meaning of sharḥ (493–494)","author":"tibi","on":["K094.02"],"anchor":"K094.02","surah":94,"ar":"قوله: (وعن الحسن: ملئ حكمة وعلما)، لعله يشير إلى ما رويناه عن البخاري ومسلم والترمذي والنسائي، عن مالك بن صعصعة، عن النبي? : \"بينا أنا عند البيت بين النائم واليقظان، فأتيت بطست من ذهب فيها ماء زمزم، فشرح صدري إلى كذا وكذا. قال قتادة: يعني لأنس: ما يعني؟ قال: إلى أسفل بطني، قال: فاستخرج قلبي فغسل بماء زمزم، ثم أعيد مكانه، ثم حشي إيمانا وحكمة، ثم أتي بدابة دون البغل وفوق الحمار\" الحديث بطوله.\n\nقال الإمام: \"لا يبعد أن يكون حصول الدم الأسود الذي غسلوه من قلبه صلوات الله عليه، علامة الميل والركون إلى المعاصي والتحجم عن الطاعات، فإذا أزالوه عنه كان ذلك علامة كون صاحبة مواظبا على الطاعات محترزا عن السيئات، يفعل الله ما يشاء ويحكم ما يريد\". الراغب: \" أصل الشرح بسط اللحم ونحوه، يقال: شرحت اللحم وشرحته، ومنه شرح الصدر، وهو بسطه بنور إلهي وسكينة من جهة الله وروح منه\".","html":"<p>On al-Ḥasan&#x27;s saying, “It was filled with wisdom and knowledge”: perhaps he alludes to what we have transmitted from al-Bukhārī, Muslim, al-Tirmidhī, and al-Nasāʾī, from Mālik <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(مَالِك)</bdi> ibn Ṣaʿṣaʿa, from the Prophet: “While I was beside the House, between sleeping and waking, a basin of gold containing Zamzam water was brought to me, and my breast was opened as far as such-and-such.” Qatāda said—addressing Anas—“What does he mean?” He replied: “To the bottom of my belly.” He continued: “My heart was taken out and washed with Zamzam water, then put back in its place, then filled with faith and wisdom. Then an animal smaller than a mule and larger than a donkey was brought to me”—the lengthy report continues.\nThe Imām [Fakhr al-Dīn al-Rāzī] said: “It is not unlikely that the black blood they washed from his heart was a sign of inclination and readiness to commit sins, and reluctance to perform acts of obedience. Once they removed it, that was a sign that its possessor would persevere in obedience and guard against evil deeds. God does whatever He wills and decrees whatever He wishes.”\nAl-Rāghib said: “The original sense of sharḥ <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(شرح)</bdi> is spreading out flesh and the like. One says sharaḥtu <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(شرحت)</bdi> al-laḥma <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(اللحمة)</bdi> and sharraḥtuhu <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(شرّحته)</bdi>. From this comes expansion of the breast: its spreading out through divine light, tranquility from God, and a spirit from Him.”</p>","lemma":"وعن الحسن: ملئ حكمة وعلما"},{"id":"T094.04","heading":"T094.04 — The reading nashraḥa (494–495)","text":"On “he read a-lam nashraḥa, with fatḥa on the ḥāʾ”: its original form is nashraḥan. The nūn was omitted and the fatḥa on the ḥāʾ retained as an indication of it. This is in al-Muntaqā.\nIbn Jinnī said: “A-lam nashraḥa, with fatḥa on the ḥāʾ, is transmitted from Abū Jaʿfar al-Manṣūr. Ibn Mujāhid said: ‘This is wholly impermissible.’” Ibn Jinnī said: “The obvious position, and what accords with customary usage, is as Ibn Mujāhid states. Something like it nevertheless occurs in what I read to Abū ʿAlī in Abū Zayd's Nawādir:\n‘From which of death's two days shall I flee—\na day not decreed, or a day decreed?’\nIt is said that he intended lam yuqdar-an, with the light emphatic nūn. In our view omitting it is impermissible, because the emphatic nūn is most closely allied to expansion and elaboration, not concision and abbreviation. In Abū Zayd's Nawādir there is another verse, said to be fabricated:\n‘Strike away the anxieties that visit you by night,\nas with a sword you strike a horse's forelock.’\nHe intended iḍrib-an, with the light nūn, and omitted it.”","title":"The reading nashraḥa (494–495)","author":"tibi","on":["K094.02"],"anchor":"K094.02","surah":94,"ar":"قوله: (قرأ: \"ألم نشرح\" بفتح الحاء)، أصله: \"نشرحن\"، فحذف وأبقى فتحة الحاء دليلا على النون في \"المنتقى\"، قال ابن جنى: \"رويت عن أبي جعفر المنصور: \"ألم نشرح\"، بفتح الحاء، قال ابن مجاهد: \"هذا غير جائز أصلا\". وقال ابن جني: \"ظاهر الأمر ومألوف الاستعمال ما ذكره ابن مجاهد، لكن جاء مثل هذا فيما قرأت على أبي علي في نوادر أبي زيد:\n\nمن أي يومي من الموت أفر أيوم لم يقدر أم يوم قدر؟\n\nقيل: أراد: لم يقدرن، بالنون الخفيفة، وحذفها عندنا غير جائز، لأن نون التأكيد أشبه شيء به الإسهاب والإطناب، لا الإيجاز والاختصار. وفي نوادر أبي زيد أيضا بيت آخر، ويقال إنه مصنوع، وهو قوله:\n\nاضرب عنك الهموم طارقها ضربك بالسيف قونس الفرس\n\nأراد: اضربن، بالنون الخفيفة، وحذفها\".","html":"<p>On “he read a-lam nashraḥa <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(نَشْرَحَ)</bdi>, with fatḥa <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(فَتْحَة (ـَ))</bdi> on the ḥāʾ <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(حاء (ح))</bdi>”: its original form is nashraḥan <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(نَشْرَحَنْ)</bdi>. The nūn <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(نون (ن))</bdi> was omitted and the fatḥa <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(فَتْحَة (ـَ))</bdi> on the ḥāʾ <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(حاء (ح))</bdi> retained as an indication of it. This is in al-Muntaqā.\nIbn Jinnī said: “A-lam nashraḥa <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(نَشْرَحَ)</bdi>, with fatḥa <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(فَتْحَة (ـَ))</bdi> on the ḥāʾ <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(حاء (ح))</bdi>, is transmitted from Abū Jaʿfar al-Manṣūr. Ibn Mujāhid said: ‘This is wholly impermissible.’” Ibn Jinnī said: “The obvious position, and what accords with customary usage, is as Ibn Mujāhid states. Something like it nevertheless occurs in what I read to Abū ʿAlī in Abū Zayd&#x27;s Nawādir:\n‘From which of death&#x27;s two days shall I flee—\na day not decreed, or a day decreed?’\nIt is said that he intended lam <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(لم)</bdi> yuqdar-an, with the light emphatic nūn <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(نون (ن))</bdi>. In our view omitting it is impermissible, because the emphatic nūn <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(نون (ن))</bdi> is most closely allied to expansion and elaboration, not concision and abbreviation. In Abū Zayd&#x27;s Nawādir there is another verse, said to be fabricated:\n‘Strike away the anxieties that visit you by night,\nas with a sword you strike a horse&#x27;s forelock.’\nHe intended iḍrib-an <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(اضربَنْ)</bdi>, with the light nūn <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(نون (ن))</bdi>, and omitted it.”</p>","lemma":"قرأ: \"ألم نشرح\" بفتح الحاء"},{"id":"T094.05","heading":"T094.05 — The burden and the ordered correspondence of its meanings (495)","text":"On “the sound of creaking and coming apart”: al-Ṣiḥāḥ says: “Anqaḍa al-ḥimlu ẓahrahu means that the load weighed upon his back. Its original sense concerns sound. Naqīḍ is the sound of camel-litters and riding gear.” Al-Rāghib says: “‘It made his back creak’ means that it broke it until it produced a creaking sound; the naqīḍ of joints is their sound. ‘Back’ is a metaphor, likening sins to a load that weighs its bearer down.”\nOn “lifting it from him means forgiving him”: the expression consists of a subject and predicate. This sentence is coordinated with a corresponding sentence, “the burden is a figure”—that is, a metaphor preceded by a comparison. “And We lifted” is then a reinforcement of the metaphor (tarshīḥ), because it is a description appropriate to that from which the metaphor is drawn. This is what he means by “lifting it from him means forgiving him,” and the rest.\nWhen “burden” is borrowed for sin, its reinforcement is appropriately understood as forgiveness. When it is borrowed for ignorance of rulings, the fitting explanation is instruction in the revealed laws. When it is understood as his desperate eagerness for their acceptance of Islam, the corresponding interpretation is the establishment of his excuse: do not be consumed by eagerness for their guidance or let yourself perish in grief over them, for you have exerted yourself in conveying the message and have established the proof against them. This is the rhetorical pattern of gathering and ordered distribution (laff wa-nashr).","title":"The burden and the ordered correspondence of its meanings (495)","author":"tibi","on":["K094.02"],"anchor":"K094.02","surah":94,"ar":"قوله: (وهو صوت الانتقاض والانفكاك)، وفي \"الصحاح\": \"أنقض الحمل ظهره، أي: أثقله. وأصله الصوت، والنقيض: صوت المحامل والرحال\".\n\nالراغب: \"أنقض ظهره: أي: كسره حتى صار له نقيض، ونقيض المفاصل صوتها. والظهر استعارة تشبيها للذنوب بالحمل الذي ينوء بحامله\".\n\nقوله: (ووضعه عنه: أن غفر له)، مبتدأ وخبر، والجملة معطوفة على مثلها وهي قوله: \"والوزر مثل\"، أي: استعارة مسبوقة بالتشبيه، فيكون {ووضعنا} ترشيحا لها، لأنه وصف مناسب للمستعار منه. هذا هو المعنى بقوله: \"ووضعه عنه: أن غفر له\" إلى آخره؛ فإذا استعير الوزر للذنب، فالمناسب أن يحمل الترشيح على معنى الغفران، وإذا استعير للجهل بالأحكام، فالملائم أن يجري على تعليم الشرائع، وإذا حمل على تهالكه صلوات الله عليه على إسلامهم، فالموافق أن يتأول بتمهيد العذر، أي: لا تحرص على هداهم، ولا تذهب نفسك عليهم حسرات، لأنك بالغت في التبليغ، وألزمت عليهم الحجة، ففيه لف ونشر.","html":"<p>On “the sound of creaking and coming apart”: al-Ṣiḥāḥ says: “Anqaḍa al-ḥimlu ẓahrahu <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(أنقض الحمل ظهره)</bdi> means that the load weighed upon his back. Its original sense concerns sound. Naqīḍ <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(نقيض)</bdi> is the sound of camel-litters and riding gear.” Al-Rāghib says: “‘It made his back creak’ means that it broke it until it produced a creaking sound; the naqīḍ <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(نقيض)</bdi> of joints is their sound. ‘Back’ is a metaphor, likening sins to a load that weighs its bearer down.”\nOn “lifting it from him means forgiving him”: the expression consists of a subject and predicate. This sentence is coordinated with a corresponding sentence, “the burden is a figure”—that is, a metaphor preceded by a comparison. “And We lifted” is then a reinforcement of the metaphor (tarshīḥ <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(ترشيح)</bdi>), because it is a description appropriate to that from which the metaphor is drawn. This is what he means by “lifting it from him means forgiving him,” and the rest.\nWhen “burden” is borrowed for sin, its reinforcement is appropriately understood as forgiveness. When it is borrowed for ignorance of rulings, the fitting explanation is instruction in the revealed laws. When it is understood as his desperate eagerness for their acceptance of Islam, the corresponding interpretation is the establishment of his excuse: do not be consumed by eagerness for their guidance or let yourself perish in grief over them, for you have exerted yourself in conveying the message and have established the proof against them. This is the rhetorical pattern of gathering and ordered distribution (laff wa-nashr).</p>","lemma":"وهو صوت الانتقاض والانفكاك"}],"cairo_notes":[],"verses":[{"key":"94:1","number":1,"ar":"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَـٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ أَلَمْ نَشْرَحْ لَكَ صَدْرَكَ","en":"Have We not caused thy bosom to dilate"},{"key":"94:2","number":2,"ar":"وَوَضَعْنَا عَنكَ وِزْرَكَ","en":"And eased thee of the burden"},{"key":"94:3","number":3,"ar":"ٱلَّذِىٓ أَنقَضَ ظَهْرَكَ","en":"Which weighed down thy back"},{"key":"94:4","number":4,"ar":"وَرَفَعْنَا لَكَ ذِكْرَكَ","en":"And exalted thy fame"}],"quran_ar":"بِسْمِ ٱللَّهِ ٱلرَّحْمَـٰنِ ٱلرَّحِيمِ أَلَمْ نَشْرَحْ لَكَ صَدْرَكَ ﴿1﴾ وَوَضَعْنَا عَنكَ وِزْرَكَ ﴿2﴾ ٱلَّذِىٓ أَنقَضَ ظَهْرَكَ ﴿3﴾ وَرَفَعْنَا لَكَ ذِكْرَكَ ﴿4﴾","quran_en":"1. Have We not caused thy bosom to dilate 2. And eased thee of the burden 3. Which weighed down thy back 4. And exalted thy fame"},{"id":"K094.03","q":"94:4","s":94,"surah":"al-Sharḥ","surah_ar":"الشرح","title":"al-Sharḥ · Raising his renown; clarification after an initial general expression","cairo":"K IV:770–772 · T XVI:492–503","source_page_first":770,"source_page_last":772,"chunk":"passages-094","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Raising his renown; clarification after an initial general expression","html":"<p>Raising his renown consists in joining his mention to God&#x27;s in the testimony of faith, the call to prayer, the announcement that prayer is beginning, the tashahhud, and sermons; and in more than one place in the Qurʾān: “God and His Messenger have the greater right that they should please Him” (9:62); “whoever obeys God and His Messenger” (4:13); and “obey God and obey the Messenger” (5:92). It also consists in calling him “God&#x27;s Messenger” and “God&#x27;s Prophet.” His mention in the scriptures of earlier peoples, and the pledge taken from the prophets and their communities that they would believe in him, belong to this as well.\nIf you ask what benefit there is in adding “for you” (laka <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(لك)</bdi>), when the meaning stands without it, I answer: the addition achieves what is achieved by initially leaving something unspecified and then clarifying it. It is as though He said, “Have We not expanded for you…?” One understands that something has been expanded. He then says, “your breast,” clarifying what had been understood only generally. The same applies to “for you your renown” and “from you your burden.”</p>","author":"kashshaf","source_id":"K094.03"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Readings, renown, and the prophetic covenant (496)","html":"<p>On “Anas read wa-ḥalalnā <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(وحللنا)</bdi> and wa-ḥaṭaṭnā <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(وحططنا)</bdi>”: Ibn Jinnī reports that Abān said: “I said to Anas, ‘Abū Ḥamza, what of wa-waḍaʿnā <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(ووضعنا)</bdi>?’ He said: ‘Waḍaʿnā <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(وضعنا)</bdi>, ḥalalnā <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(حللنا)</bdi>, and ḥaṭaṭnā <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(حططنا)</bdi> are equivalent. Gabriel came to the Prophet and said: Recite according to seven aḥruf <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(أحرف)</bdi>, provided you do not mix forgiveness with punishment or punishment with forgiveness.’”\nI say: a lengthy report from Anas occurs in Muslim, al-Tirmidhī, Abū Dāwūd, and al-Nasāʾī, ending: “Then he said: Each of them is healing and sufficient—whether you say ‘All-Hearing, All-Knowing’ or ‘Mighty, Wise’—provided you do not conclude a verse of punishment with mercy or a verse of mercy with punishment.” [These are Ṭībī&#x27;s transmitted attributions.]\nOn “calling him God&#x27;s Messenger and God&#x27;s Prophet”: Jaʿfar said: “No one mentions you as messenger without mentioning Me as Lord.” Ibn ʿAṭāʾ said: “I made faith in Me complete through mentioning you together with Me.”\nOn “the pledge taken from the prophets and their communities that they would believe in him”: perhaps he means what is indicated by “When God took the covenant of the prophets: Whatever scripture and wisdom I give you, then a messenger comes to you confirming what is with you, you shall believe in him and you shall support him” (3:81).</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T094.06"},{"type":"exposition","title":"The added laka and the connection with consolation (497)","html":"<p>On “the addition of ‘for you’”: the author said: “Laka <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(لك)</bdi> may be added to express particularization, as in ‘You alone we worship’ (1:5), although the meaning is complete with ‘we worship You’; or it may be a matter of giving priority to what is more important.”\nAl-Sayyid Ibn al-Shajarī says in al-Amālī: “The lām <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(لام (ل))</bdi> in laka <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(لك)</bdi> is causal, as when you say, ‘I did that to honor you.’ If you omit the lām <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(لام (ل))</bdi>, you say, ‘I did it, honoring you.’ If you omit the verbal noun, you restore the lām <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(لام (ل))</bdi> and say, ‘I did that for you.’ Thus the meaning is: Have We not expanded your breast for your guidance? Compare: ‘Whomever God wishes to guide, He expands his breast for Islam’ (6:125). Once the verbal noun is omitted, the lām <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(لام (ل))</bdi> must be expressed. Likewise, ‘We raised your renown for you’ means: We raised your renown to honor you.”\nOn “the idolaters used to taunt”: his explanation amounts to this. The occasion of revelation of “Have We not expanded your breast for you?” was that the idolaters taunted God&#x27;s Messenger and the believers for poverty, causing him concern. This was dispelled by “Have We not expanded your breast for you?” The question repudiates the denial of expansion, thereby emphatically establishing it: have you not seen what God did for you at the outset—expanding your breast and raising your renown—when you knew nothing of what you know now and your name was then obscure? We did for you what We did. Judge what is to come by that, and do not worry about their taunting you and the believers for poverty, for with hardship comes ease.</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T094.07"}],"types":["exposition","language","theology","reports"],"ar":"ورفع ذكره: أن قرن بذكر الله في كلمة الشهادة والأذان والإقامة والتشهد والخطب، وفي غير موضع من القرآن والله ورسوله أحق أن يرضوه، ومن يطع الله ورسوله، وأطيعوا الله وأطيعوا الرسول وفي تسميته رسول الله ونبى الله، ومنه ذكره في كتب الأولين، والأخذ على الأنبياء وأممهم أن يؤمنوا به. فإن قلت: أى فائدة في زيادة لك، والمعنى مستقل بدونه ؟ قلت: في زيادة لك ما في طريقة \nالإبهام والإيضاح، كأنه قيل: ألم نشرح لك، ففهم أن ثم مشروحا، ثم قيل: صدرك، فأوضح ما علم مبهما، وكذلك لك ذكرك وعنك وزرك.\nقوله: (وقرأ أنس: \"وحللنا وحططنا\")، عن ابن جني، \"قال أبان: قلت لأنس: يا أبا حمزة: {ووضعنا}؟ قال: \"وضعنا\" و\"حللنا\" و\"حططنا\" سواء. إن جبريل عليه السلام أتى النبي? ، قال: اقرأ على سبعة أحرف، ما لا تخلط مغفرة بعذاب، وعذابا بمغفرة\".\nقلت: قد جاء عن مسلم والترمذي وأبي داود والنسائي، عن أنس في حديث طويل، وفي آخره: \"ثم قال: ليس منها إلا شاف كاف؛ إن قلت: سميعا عليما عزيزا حكيما، ما لم تختم آية عذاب برحمة، أو آية رحن بعذاب\".\nقوله: (وفي تسميته رسول الله ونبي الله)، قال جعفر: \"لا يذكرك أحد بالرسالة إلا ذكرني بالربوبية، وقال ابن عطاء: جعلت تمام الإيمان بي بذكرك معي\".\nقوله: (والأخذ على الأنبياء وأممهم أن يؤمنوا به)، لعله أراد ما دل عليه قوله تعالى: {وإذ أخذ الله ميثاق النبيين لما آتيتكم من كتاب وحكمة ثم جاءكم رسول مصدق لما معكم لتؤمنن به ولتنصرنه} [آل عمران: 81].\nقوله: (في زيادة {لك}). قال المصنف رحمه الله: \"يحتمل أن يكون {لك} زيادة للاختصاص، كما في {إياك نعبد} [الفاتحة: 5]، وإن كان المعنى مستقلا ب \"نعبدك\"، وأن يكون من قبيل الأهم فالأهم\".\nوقال السيد ابن الشجري في \"الأمالي\": \"اللام في {لك} لام العلة، نحو قولك: فعلت ذلك لإكرامك، فإن حذفتها قلت: فعلته إكرامك، وإن حذفت المصدر رددت اللام فقلت: فعلت ذلك لك؛ فالمعنى: ألم نشرح لهداك صدرك؟ كما قال تعالى: {فمن يرد الله أن يهديه يشرح صدره للإسلام} [الأنعام: 125]، فلما حذف المصدر وجب إثبات اللام. وكذلك قوله: \"ورفعنا لك ذكرك\"، أي: رفعنا لتشريفك ذكرك\".\nقوله: (كان المشركون يعيرون)، تلخيصه: أن قوله: {ألم نشرح لك صدرك}، سبب نزوله أن المشركين كانوا يعيرون رسول الله? والمؤمنين بالفقر، فاهتم لذلك رسول الله? ، فأزيل ذلك بقوله: {ألم نشرح لك صدرك}، فدل الاستفهام على إنكار نفي الانشراح مبالغة في إثباته، يعني: ألم تر كيف فعل الله بك في بدء أمرك من انشراح الصدر والرفع من الذكر، وأنت غير عالم حينئذ بشيء مما تعلمه الآن، وأنت يومئذ خامل الذكر، ففعلنا بك ما فعلنا، فقس على ذلك ولا تهتم بتعييرهم لك وللمؤمنين بالفقر، فإن مع العسر يسرا.","layers":[{"id":"K094.03","heading":"K094.03 — Raising his renown; clarification after an initial general expression","text":"Raising his renown consists in joining his mention to God's in the testimony of faith, the call to prayer, the announcement that prayer is beginning, the tashahhud, and sermons; and in more than one place in the Qurʾān: “God and His Messenger have the greater right that they should please Him” (9:62); “whoever obeys God and His Messenger” (4:13); and “obey God and obey the Messenger” (5:92). It also consists in calling him “God's Messenger” and “God's Prophet.” His mention in the scriptures of earlier peoples, and the pledge taken from the prophets and their communities that they would believe in him, belong to this as well.\nIf you ask what benefit there is in adding “for you” (laka), when the meaning stands without it, I answer: the addition achieves what is achieved by initially leaving something unspecified and then clarifying it. It is as though He said, “Have We not expanded for you…?” One understands that something has been expanded. He then says, “your breast,” clarifying what had been understood only generally. The same applies to “for you your renown” and “from you your burden.”","title":"Raising his renown; clarification after an initial general expression","author":"kashshaf","surah":94,"ar":"ورفع ذكره: أن قرن بذكر الله في كلمة الشهادة والأذان والإقامة والتشهد والخطب، وفي غير موضع من القرآن والله ورسوله أحق أن يرضوه، ومن يطع الله ورسوله، وأطيعوا الله وأطيعوا الرسول وفي تسميته رسول الله ونبى الله، ومنه ذكره في كتب الأولين، والأخذ على الأنبياء وأممهم أن يؤمنوا به. فإن قلت: أى فائدة في زيادة لك، والمعنى مستقل بدونه ؟ قلت: في زيادة لك ما في طريقة \n\nالإبهام والإيضاح، كأنه قيل: ألم نشرح لك، ففهم أن ثم مشروحا، ثم قيل: صدرك، فأوضح ما علم مبهما، وكذلك لك ذكرك وعنك وزرك.","html":"<p>Raising his renown consists in joining his mention to God&#x27;s in the testimony of faith, the call to prayer, the announcement that prayer is beginning, the tashahhud, and sermons; and in more than one place in the Qurʾān: “God and His Messenger have the greater right that they should please Him” (9:62); “whoever obeys God and His Messenger” (4:13); and “obey God and obey the Messenger” (5:92). It also consists in calling him “God&#x27;s Messenger” and “God&#x27;s Prophet.” His mention in the scriptures of earlier peoples, and the pledge taken from the prophets and their communities that they would believe in him, belong to this as well.\nIf you ask what benefit there is in adding “for you” (laka <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(لك)</bdi>), when the meaning stands without it, I answer: the addition achieves what is achieved by initially leaving something unspecified and then clarifying it. It is as though He said, “Have We not expanded for you…?” One understands that something has been expanded. He then says, “your breast,” clarifying what had been understood only generally. The same applies to “for you your renown” and “from you your burden.”</p>","lemma":""},{"id":"T094.06","heading":"T094.06 — Readings, renown, and the prophetic covenant (496)","text":"On “Anas read wa-ḥalalnā and wa-ḥaṭaṭnā”: Ibn Jinnī reports that Abān said: “I said to Anas, ‘Abū Ḥamza, what of wa-waḍaʿnā?’ He said: ‘Waḍaʿnā, ḥalalnā, and ḥaṭaṭnā are equivalent. Gabriel came to the Prophet and said: Recite according to seven aḥruf, provided you do not mix forgiveness with punishment or punishment with forgiveness.’”\nI say: a lengthy report from Anas occurs in Muslim, al-Tirmidhī, Abū Dāwūd, and al-Nasāʾī, ending: “Then he said: Each of them is healing and sufficient—whether you say ‘All-Hearing, All-Knowing’ or ‘Mighty, Wise’—provided you do not conclude a verse of punishment with mercy or a verse of mercy with punishment.” [These are Ṭībī's transmitted attributions.]\nOn “calling him God's Messenger and God's Prophet”: Jaʿfar said: “No one mentions you as messenger without mentioning Me as Lord.” Ibn ʿAṭāʾ said: “I made faith in Me complete through mentioning you together with Me.”\nOn “the pledge taken from the prophets and their communities that they would believe in him”: perhaps he means what is indicated by “When God took the covenant of the prophets: Whatever scripture and wisdom I give you, then a messenger comes to you confirming what is with you, you shall believe in him and you shall support him” (3:81).","title":"Readings, renown, and the prophetic covenant (496)","author":"tibi","on":["K094.03"],"anchor":"K094.03","surah":94,"ar":"قوله: (وقرأ أنس: \"وحللنا وحططنا\")، عن ابن جني، \"قال أبان: قلت لأنس: يا أبا حمزة: {ووضعنا}؟ قال: \"وضعنا\" و\"حللنا\" و\"حططنا\" سواء. إن جبريل عليه السلام أتى النبي? ، قال: اقرأ على سبعة أحرف، ما لا تخلط مغفرة بعذاب، وعذابا بمغفرة\".\n\nقلت: قد جاء عن مسلم والترمذي وأبي داود والنسائي، عن أنس في حديث طويل، وفي آخره: \"ثم قال: ليس منها إلا شاف كاف؛ إن قلت: سميعا عليما عزيزا حكيما، ما لم تختم آية عذاب برحمة، أو آية رحن بعذاب\".\n\nقوله: (وفي تسميته رسول الله ونبي الله)، قال جعفر: \"لا يذكرك أحد بالرسالة إلا ذكرني بالربوبية، وقال ابن عطاء: جعلت تمام الإيمان بي بذكرك معي\".\n\nقوله: (والأخذ على الأنبياء وأممهم أن يؤمنوا به)، لعله أراد ما دل عليه قوله تعالى: {وإذ أخذ الله ميثاق النبيين لما آتيتكم من كتاب وحكمة ثم جاءكم رسول مصدق لما معكم لتؤمنن به ولتنصرنه} [آل عمران: 81].","html":"<p>On “Anas read wa-ḥalalnā <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(وحللنا)</bdi> and wa-ḥaṭaṭnā <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(وحططنا)</bdi>”: Ibn Jinnī reports that Abān said: “I said to Anas, ‘Abū Ḥamza, what of wa-waḍaʿnā <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(ووضعنا)</bdi>?’ He said: ‘Waḍaʿnā <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(وضعنا)</bdi>, ḥalalnā <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(حللنا)</bdi>, and ḥaṭaṭnā <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(حططنا)</bdi> are equivalent. Gabriel came to the Prophet and said: Recite according to seven aḥruf <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(أحرف)</bdi>, provided you do not mix forgiveness with punishment or punishment with forgiveness.’”\nI say: a lengthy report from Anas occurs in Muslim, al-Tirmidhī, Abū Dāwūd, and al-Nasāʾī, ending: “Then he said: Each of them is healing and sufficient—whether you say ‘All-Hearing, All-Knowing’ or ‘Mighty, Wise’—provided you do not conclude a verse of punishment with mercy or a verse of mercy with punishment.” [These are Ṭībī&#x27;s transmitted attributions.]\nOn “calling him God&#x27;s Messenger and God&#x27;s Prophet”: Jaʿfar said: “No one mentions you as messenger without mentioning Me as Lord.” Ibn ʿAṭāʾ said: “I made faith in Me complete through mentioning you together with Me.”\nOn “the pledge taken from the prophets and their communities that they would believe in him”: perhaps he means what is indicated by “When God took the covenant of the prophets: Whatever scripture and wisdom I give you, then a messenger comes to you confirming what is with you, you shall believe in him and you shall support him” (3:81).</p>","lemma":"وقرأ أنس: \"وحللنا وحططنا\""},{"id":"T094.07","heading":"T094.07 — The added laka and the connection with consolation (497)","text":"On “the addition of ‘for you’”: the author said: “Laka may be added to express particularization, as in ‘You alone we worship’ (1:5), although the meaning is complete with ‘we worship You’; or it may be a matter of giving priority to what is more important.”\nAl-Sayyid Ibn al-Shajarī says in al-Amālī: “The lām in laka is causal, as when you say, ‘I did that to honor you.’ If you omit the lām, you say, ‘I did it, honoring you.’ If you omit the verbal noun, you restore the lām and say, ‘I did that for you.’ Thus the meaning is: Have We not expanded your breast for your guidance? Compare: ‘Whomever God wishes to guide, He expands his breast for Islam’ (6:125). Once the verbal noun is omitted, the lām must be expressed. Likewise, ‘We raised your renown for you’ means: We raised your renown to honor you.”\nOn “the idolaters used to taunt”: his explanation amounts to this. The occasion of revelation of “Have We not expanded your breast for you?” was that the idolaters taunted God's Messenger and the believers for poverty, causing him concern. This was dispelled by “Have We not expanded your breast for you?” The question repudiates the denial of expansion, thereby emphatically establishing it: have you not seen what God did for you at the outset—expanding your breast and raising your renown—when you knew nothing of what you know now and your name was then obscure? We did for you what We did. Judge what is to come by that, and do not worry about their taunting you and the believers for poverty, for with hardship comes ease.","title":"The added laka and the connection with consolation (497)","author":"tibi","on":["K094.03"],"anchor":"K094.03","surah":94,"ar":"قوله: (في زيادة {لك}). قال المصنف رحمه الله: \"يحتمل أن يكون {لك} زيادة للاختصاص، كما في {إياك نعبد} [الفاتحة: 5]، وإن كان المعنى مستقلا ب \"نعبدك\"، وأن يكون من قبيل الأهم فالأهم\".\n\nوقال السيد ابن الشجري في \"الأمالي\": \"اللام في {لك} لام العلة، نحو قولك: فعلت ذلك لإكرامك، فإن حذفتها قلت: فعلته إكرامك، وإن حذفت المصدر رددت اللام فقلت: فعلت ذلك لك؛ فالمعنى: ألم نشرح لهداك صدرك؟ كما قال تعالى: {فمن يرد الله أن يهديه يشرح صدره للإسلام} [الأنعام: 125]، فلما حذف المصدر وجب إثبات اللام. وكذلك قوله: \"ورفعنا لك ذكرك\"، أي: رفعنا لتشريفك ذكرك\".\n\nقوله: (كان المشركون يعيرون)، تلخيصه: أن قوله: {ألم نشرح لك صدرك}، سبب نزوله أن المشركين كانوا يعيرون رسول الله? والمؤمنين بالفقر، فاهتم لذلك رسول الله? ، فأزيل ذلك بقوله: {ألم نشرح لك صدرك}، فدل الاستفهام على إنكار نفي الانشراح مبالغة في إثباته، يعني: ألم تر كيف فعل الله بك في بدء أمرك من انشراح الصدر والرفع من الذكر، وأنت غير عالم حينئذ بشيء مما تعلمه الآن، وأنت يومئذ خامل الذكر، ففعلنا بك ما فعلنا، فقس على ذلك ولا تهتم بتعييرهم لك وللمؤمنين بالفقر، فإن مع العسر يسرا.","html":"<p>On “the addition of ‘for you’”: the author said: “Laka <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(لك)</bdi> may be added to express particularization, as in ‘You alone we worship’ (1:5), although the meaning is complete with ‘we worship You’; or it may be a matter of giving priority to what is more important.”\nAl-Sayyid Ibn al-Shajarī says in al-Amālī: “The lām <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(لام (ل))</bdi> in laka <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(لك)</bdi> is causal, as when you say, ‘I did that to honor you.’ If you omit the lām <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(لام (ل))</bdi>, you say, ‘I did it, honoring you.’ If you omit the verbal noun, you restore the lām <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(لام (ل))</bdi> and say, ‘I did that for you.’ Thus the meaning is: Have We not expanded your breast for your guidance? Compare: ‘Whomever God wishes to guide, He expands his breast for Islam’ (6:125). Once the verbal noun is omitted, the lām <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(لام (ل))</bdi> must be expressed. Likewise, ‘We raised your renown for you’ means: We raised your renown to honor you.”\nOn “the idolaters used to taunt”: his explanation amounts to this. The occasion of revelation of “Have We not expanded your breast for you?” was that the idolaters taunted God&#x27;s Messenger and the believers for poverty, causing him concern. This was dispelled by “Have We not expanded your breast for you?” The question repudiates the denial of expansion, thereby emphatically establishing it: have you not seen what God did for you at the outset—expanding your breast and raising your renown—when you knew nothing of what you know now and your name was then obscure? We did for you what We did. Judge what is to come by that, and do not worry about their taunting you and the believers for poverty, for with hardship comes ease.</p>","lemma":"في زيادة {لك}). قال المصنف رحمه الله: \"يحتمل أن يكون {لك"}],"cairo_notes":[],"verses":[{"key":"94:4","number":4,"ar":"وَرَفَعْنَا لَكَ ذِكْرَكَ","en":"And exalted thy fame"}],"quran_ar":"وَرَفَعْنَا لَكَ ذِكْرَكَ ﴿4﴾","quran_en":"4. And exalted thy fame"},{"id":"K094.04","q":"94:5–6","s":94,"surah":"al-Sharḥ","surah_ar":"الشرح","title":"al-Sharḥ · Verses 5–6: hardship and ease","cairo":"K IV:770–772 · T XVI:492–503","source_page_first":770,"source_page_last":772,"chunk":"passages-094","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Verses 5–6: hardship and ease","html":"<p>“For with hardship comes ease; indeed, with hardship comes ease.”\nIf you ask how “for with hardship comes ease” connects with what precedes, I answer: the idolaters used to taunt God&#x27;s Messenger and the believers for their poverty and straitened circumstances, until it occurred to him that their aversion to Islam arose from its adherents&#x27; poverty and their contempt for them. God reminded him of the great blessings He had bestowed upon him, then said, “For with hardship comes ease.” It is as though He said: We have granted you all that We have granted you, so do not despair of God&#x27;s bounty; with the hardship in which you now find yourselves comes ease.\nIf you ask: maʿa <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(مع)</bdi> means accompaniment; how can ease and hardship accompany one another? I answer: He means that God will bring them ease a short time after the hardship in which they were living. He brings the anticipated ease so near that He makes it seem simultaneous with the hardship, to give further consolation and strengthen their hearts.</p>","author":"kashshaf","source_id":"K094.04"}],"types":["exposition","language","theology","reports"],"ar":"فإن مع العسر يسرا (5) إن مع العسر يسرا (6)\nفإن قلت: كيف تعلق قوله فإن مع العسر يسرا بما قبله؟ قلت: كان المشركون يعيرون رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم والمؤمنين بالفقر والضيقة، حتى سبق إلى وهمه أنهم رغبوا عن الإسلام لافتقار أهله واحتقارهم، فذكره ما أنعم به عليه من جلائل النعم ثم قال:\nفإن مع العسر يسرا كأنه قال: خولناك ما خولناك فلا تيأس من فضل الله، فإن مع العسر الذي أنتم فيه يسرا. فإن قلت: إن مع للصحبة، فما معنى اصطحاب اليسر والعسر؟ قلت:\nأراد أن الله يصيبهم بيسر بعد العسر الذي كانوا فيه بزمان قريب، فقرب اليسر المترقب حتى جعله كالمقارن للعسر، زيادة في التسلية وتقوية القلوب.","layers":[{"id":"K094.04","heading":"K094.04 — Verses 5–6: hardship and ease","text":"“For with hardship comes ease; indeed, with hardship comes ease.”\nIf you ask how “for with hardship comes ease” connects with what precedes, I answer: the idolaters used to taunt God's Messenger and the believers for their poverty and straitened circumstances, until it occurred to him that their aversion to Islam arose from its adherents' poverty and their contempt for them. God reminded him of the great blessings He had bestowed upon him, then said, “For with hardship comes ease.” It is as though He said: We have granted you all that We have granted you, so do not despair of God's bounty; with the hardship in which you now find yourselves comes ease.\nIf you ask: maʿa means accompaniment; how can ease and hardship accompany one another? I answer: He means that God will bring them ease a short time after the hardship in which they were living. He brings the anticipated ease so near that He makes it seem simultaneous with the hardship, to give further consolation and strengthen their hearts.","title":"Verses 5–6: hardship and ease","author":"kashshaf","surah":94,"ar":"فإن مع العسر يسرا (5) إن مع العسر يسرا (6)\n\nفإن قلت: كيف تعلق قوله فإن مع العسر يسرا بما قبله؟ قلت: كان المشركون يعيرون رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم والمؤمنين بالفقر والضيقة، حتى سبق إلى وهمه أنهم رغبوا عن الإسلام لافتقار أهله واحتقارهم، فذكره ما أنعم به عليه من جلائل النعم ثم قال:\n\nفإن مع العسر يسرا كأنه قال: خولناك ما خولناك فلا تيأس من فضل الله، فإن مع العسر الذي أنتم فيه يسرا. فإن قلت: إن مع للصحبة، فما معنى اصطحاب اليسر والعسر؟ قلت:\n\nأراد أن الله يصيبهم بيسر بعد العسر الذي كانوا فيه بزمان قريب، فقرب اليسر المترقب حتى جعله كالمقارن للعسر، زيادة في التسلية وتقوية القلوب.","html":"<p>“For with hardship comes ease; indeed, with hardship comes ease.”\nIf you ask how “for with hardship comes ease” connects with what precedes, I answer: the idolaters used to taunt God&#x27;s Messenger and the believers for their poverty and straitened circumstances, until it occurred to him that their aversion to Islam arose from its adherents&#x27; poverty and their contempt for them. God reminded him of the great blessings He had bestowed upon him, then said, “For with hardship comes ease.” It is as though He said: We have granted you all that We have granted you, so do not despair of God&#x27;s bounty; with the hardship in which you now find yourselves comes ease.\nIf you ask: maʿa <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(مع)</bdi> means accompaniment; how can ease and hardship accompany one another? I answer: He means that God will bring them ease a short time after the hardship in which they were living. He brings the anticipated ease so near that He makes it seem simultaneous with the hardship, to give further consolation and strengthen their hearts.</p>","lemma":""}],"cairo_notes":[],"verses":[{"key":"94:5","number":5,"ar":"فَإِنَّ مَعَ ٱلْعُسْرِ يُسْرًا","en":"But lo! with hardship goeth ease"},{"key":"94:6","number":6,"ar":"إِنَّ مَعَ ٱلْعُسْرِ يُسْرًا","en":"Lo! with hardship goeth ease"}],"quran_ar":"فَإِنَّ مَعَ ٱلْعُسْرِ يُسْرًا ﴿5﴾ إِنَّ مَعَ ٱلْعُسْرِ يُسْرًا ﴿6﴾","quran_en":"5. But lo! with hardship goeth ease 6. Lo! with hardship goeth ease"},{"id":"K094.05","q":"94:5–6","s":94,"surah":"al-Sharḥ","surah_ar":"الشرح","title":"al-Sharḥ · “One hardship will not overcome two eases”","cairo":"K IV:770–772 · T XVI:492–503","source_page_first":770,"source_page_last":772,"chunk":"passages-094","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"“One hardship will not overcome two eases”","html":"<p>If you ask about the saying of Ibn ʿAbbās and Ibn Masʿūd, “One hardship will not overcome two eases”—also transmitted as a report traced to the Prophet, who came out one day laughing and saying, “One hardship will not overcome two eases”—I answer: this proceeds on the apparent sense and rests on strong hope, and on the principle that God&#x27;s promise should be understood only in the fullest and most ample sense that its wording can bear.\nThe explanation is that the second sentence may repeat the first, as “woe that day to those who deny” is repeated to establish its meaning in people&#x27;s minds and fix it in their hearts, or as an individual word is repeated in “Zayd came to me—Zayd.” Alternatively, the first sentence may promise that hardship will necessarily be followed by ease, and the second may be a fresh promise that hardship will be followed by ease. On the interpretation that it is a new statement, there are two eases.\nThe hardship is one because its definiteness must either refer to something already known—the hardship in which they were living—in which case it is the same hardship, just as Zayd is the same in “With Zayd there is wealth; with Zayd there is wealth”; or it refers to the class familiar to everyone, in which case it is likewise the same. Ease, however, is indefinite and denotes some member of its class. If the second statement is new rather than a repetition, it unquestionably denotes a member other than the first.\nIf you ask what the two eases mean, I answer: they may mean the conquests made easy for them in the days of God&#x27;s Messenger and those made easy in the days of the caliphs; or ease in this world and ease in the next. Compare: “Say: Do you await for us anything but one of the two good outcomes?” (9:52), namely the good of victory and the good of reward.\nIf you ask what the indefiniteness signifies, I answer: magnification—as though He said, “With hardship comes a mighty ease—and what an ease!” In Ibn Masʿūd&#x27;s codex it occurs only once. If you ask: if it is not repeated in his reading, why did he say, “By Him in whose hand is my soul, if hardship were in a hole, ease would pursue it until it entered upon it; one hardship will not overcome two eases”? I answer: by the two eases he seems to have intended the magnifying force of the indefinite “ease,” interpreting it as the ease of both abodes. Those are indeed two eases.</p>","author":"kashshaf","source_id":"K094.05"},{"type":"exposition","title":"The report and the apparent sense (498)","html":"<p>On “it has been transmitted as a report traced to the Prophet”: Mālik <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(مَالِك)</bdi> transmits in al-Muwaṭṭaʾ from Zayd ibn Aslam: “Abū ʿUbayda wrote to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, mentioning the assembled Roman forces and his apprehension concerning them. ʿUmar wrote back: To proceed: whatever hardship befalls a believing servant, God brings relief after it. One hardship will not overcome two eases.”\nOn “this proceeds on the apparent sense”: ‘apparent’ (ẓāhir <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(ظاهر)</bdi>) means wording admitting more than one interpretation, with one of them rendered more probable by a cogent indication. What they said follows the apparent sense: the revelation admits both repetition and a new statement. The indication favoring one possibility—the new statement, because it is fuller and more ample—is that God&#x27;s promise is to be understood in the fullest of the possible senses. “And on the principle that God&#x27;s promise…” is an explanatory coordination with “and rests on strong hope,” which is similarly related to “proceeds on the apparent sense.” “The explanation is…” sets out the two possibilities.</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T094.08"},{"type":"exposition","title":"How the unrepeated reading can also signify two eases (499)","html":"<p>On this basis, even without repetition—as in Ibn Masʿūd&#x27;s reading—the intended meaning is conveyed. The indefinite yusran <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(يسرًا)</bdi> can signify either some instance of ease or magnification. Since the matter rests on strong hope, the latter is preferred. The difference is that the first interpretation signifies the intended meaning by linguistic convention, as will follow, while the second signifies it by implication and indirect expression (kināya). Magnification in yusran <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(يسرًا)</bdi> requires ease to reach its utmost extent; otherwise it would not mean ease in both abodes. From this follows the multiplicity of ease, and the saying, “One hardship will not overcome two eases.” This is what he indicates by “those are indeed two eases.”\nIf this meaning is intended through repetition, it is more eloquent than treating the sentence as a fresh statement. Without the transmitted saying and prophetic report drawing attention to this subtle point, it would not be understood. One may also say: since the verse concerns the noble Companions and promises them relief after distress, it must be understood as ease in both abodes. In this world that consists of wealth after poverty, strength after weakness, and honor after humiliation. As for the next world, there is no dispute about it.</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T094.09"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Definite and indefinite repetition (499–500)","html":"<p>On “the hardship is one”: know that, according to the exact investigators, the definite article lām <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(لام (ل))</bdi> is assigned to indication and reference to something known. The author of al-Takhmīr says: “The lām <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(لام (ل))</bdi> is for indication itself. Sometimes the indication points to an individual already familiar to the addressee, and sometimes to a class. Its meaning is one in every case. Understand this: people&#x27;s errors concerning it are considerable, and it is a point of doctrinal grammatical importance.”\nI say: it consequently requires something previously available to be indicated. Whenever the discourse contains something that can serve as such a referent in any way, that is selected. Al-Bazdawī says: “The defining lām <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(لام (ل))</bdi> refers back to what is known: a thing is mentioned and then returned to, the second being the first. An example is our scholars&#x27; ruling about someone who acknowledges a thousand with a particular qualification, then acknowledges it again with the same qualification: the second is the first. If each expression is indefinite, there is disagreement over whether unity of the sitting is a condition for the second to be identical with the first. According to Abū Ḥanīfa it is; according to Abū Yūsuf it is not.”\nThe author of al-Muṭliʿ transmits from al-Farrāʾ that when the Arabs mention an indefinite noun and then repeat an indefinite noun like it, they become two: “When you earn a dirham, spend a dirham.” The second differs from the first. If they repeat it in definite form, it is the same. Al-Zajjāj states something similar.\nAl-Sayyid says in al-Amālī: “Hardship is definite and ease indefinite because, when a noun is repeated indefinitely, the second differs from the first: ‘A man came to me, and I said to a man such-and-such.’ The same applies when the first is definite and the second indefinite: ‘The man arrived, and I said to a man such-and-such.’ If the first is indefinite and the second definite, the second is the first; likewise when a definite noun follows a definite noun: ‘The man arrived, and I honored the man.’ Hence Ibn ʿAbbās said, ‘One hardship will not overcome two eases.’”</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T094.10"}],"types":["exposition","language","theology","reports"],"ar":"فإن قلت: ما معنى قول ابن عباس وابن مسعود رضى الله عنهما: لن يغلب عسر يسرين وقد روى مرفوعا أنه خرج صلى الله عليه وسلم ذات يوم وهو يضحك ويقول «لن يغلب عسر يسرين» ؟ قلت: هذا عمل على الظاهر، وبناء على قوة الرجاء، وأن موعد الله لا يحمل إلا على أو في ما يحتمله اللفظ وأبلغه، والقول في أنه يحتمل أن تكون الجملة الثانية تكريرا للأولى كما كرر قوله فويل يومئذ للمكذبين لتقرير معناها في النفوس وتمكينها في القلوب، وكما يكرر المفرد في قولك: جاءني زيد زيد، وأن تكون الأولى عدة بأن العسر مردوف بيسر لا محالة، والثانية عدة مستأنفة بأن العسر متبوع بيسر، فهما يسران على تقدير الاستئناف، وإنما كان العسر واحدا لأنه لا يخلو، إما أن يكون تعريفه للعهد وهو العسر الذي كانوا فيه، فهو هو، لأن حكمه حكم زيد في قولك:\nإن مع زيد مالا، إن مع زيد مالا. وإما أن يكون للجنس الذي يعلمه كل أحد فهو هو أيضا.\nوأما اليسر فمنكر متناول لبعض الجنس، فإذا كان الكلام الثاني متأنفا غير مكرر فقد تناول بعضا غير البعض الأول بغير إشكال. فإن قلت: فما المراد باليسرين؟ قلت: يجوز أن يراد بهما \nما تيسر لهم من الفتوح في أيام رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم وما تيسر لهم في أيام الخلفاء ، وأن يراد يسر الدنيا ويسر الآخرة، كقوله تعالى قل هل تربصون بنا إلا إحدى الحسنيين وهما حسنى الظفر وحسنى الثواب. فإن قلت فما معنى هذا التنكير؟ قلت: التفخيم، كأنه قيل إن مع العسر يسرا عظيما وأى يسر، وهو في مصحف ابن مسعود مرة واحدة. فإن قلت: فإذا ثبت في قراءته غير مكرر، فلم قال: والذي نفسي بيده، لو كان العسر في جحر لطلبه اليسر حتى يدخل عليه، إنه لن يغلب عسر يسرين ؟ قلت: كأنه قصد باليسرين: ما في قوله يسرا من معنى التفخيم، فتأوله بيسر الدارين، وذلك يسران في الحقيقة.\nقوله: (وقد روي مرفوعا)، روى مالك في \"الموطأ\" عن زيد بن أسلم، قال: \"كتب أبو عبيدة إلى عمر بن الخطاب رضي الله عنهما، يذكر له جموعا من الروم وما يتخوف منهم، فكتب إليه عمر رضي الله عنه: أما بعد، فإنه مهما ينزل بعبد مؤمن شدة، يجعل الله بعده فرجا، ولن يغلب عسر يسرين\".\nقوله: (هذا عمل على الظاهر)، والمعنى بالظاهر: اللفظ المحتمل الراجح أحد محتملاته بقرينة ناهضة، يعني: ما ذكروه عمل بالظاهر؛ فإن ما في التنزيل يحتمل التكرير والاستئناف، والقرينة التي ترجح أحد الاحتمالين، أي: الاستئناف لأنه أوفاهما وأبلغهما، هي أن مبنى \"أن موعد الله لا يحمل إلا على أوفى الاحتمالين\"، عطف تفسيري على قوله: \"وبناء على قوة الرجاء\"، وهو على \"عمل بالظاهر\" كذلك. وقوله: \"والقول فيه\" إلى آخره، بيان للاحتمالين.\nفعلى هذا، لو لم يكرر - كما هي قراءة ابن مسعود، - أفاد المراد المقصود، وذلك أن التنكير في {يسرا}، يحتمل أن يراد منه بعض من اليسر، وأن يراد منه التفخيم، ولما كان بناء الأمر على قوة الرجاء، رجح الثاني. والفرق بين هذا والأول أن دلالة الأول على المراد بالوضع كما سيجيء، ودلالة الثاني عليه باللزوم والكناية؛ فإن التفخيم في {يسرا}، اقتضى أن يتناهي في، ولو لم يكن متناهيا فيه، إذن لم يرد به يسر الدارين، ولزم من ذلك تعدد اليسر، وأن يقال: \"لن يغلب عسر يسرين\"، وإليه الإشارة بقوله: \"وذلك يسران في الحقيقة\". وإذا ذهب إلى هذا المعني في التكرير، كان أبلغ من الاستئناف، ولولا التنبيه بالأثر والحديث على هذه اللطيفة، لم يفهم ذلك. ويمكن أن يقال: لما كان ورود الآية في حق الصحابة الكرام، ووعدا لهم بالفرج بعد الشدة، أوجب أن يحمل على يسر الدارين: أما في الدنيا، فبالغنى بعد الفقر، والقوة بعد الضعف، وبالعز بعد الذل. وأما في الآخرة، فلا كلام فيه.\nقوله: \"وإنما كان العسر واحدا)، إلى آخره، اعلم أن لام التعريف عند المحقيين موضوعة للإشارة والعهد، قال صاحب \"التخمير\": \"اعلم أن اللام لنفس الإشارة، لكن الإشارة\nتقع تارة إلى فرد لمخاطبك به عهد، وأخرى إلى جنس؛ فمعنى اللام واحد على كل حال فاعرفه؛ فإن غلط الناس فيه عظيم، وهي فائدة مذهبية\".\nقلت: فإذن لا بد له من تقدم مشار إليه، فإذا جاء في الكلام ما يصلح أن يكون مشارا إليه بأي وجه كان، تعين له، قال البزدوي: \"اللام المعرفة للعهد، وهو أن يذكر شيئا ثم يعاوده، فيكون الثاني هو الأول، مثاله قول علمائنا فيمن أقر بألف مقيدا بقيد، ثم أقر به كذلك أن الثاني هو الأول، وإذا كان كل واحد منهما نكرة، جاء الخلاف في أن اتحاد المجلس شرط لأن يكون الثاني عين الأول، فعند أبي حنيفة رحمه الله: نعم، وعند أبي يوسف: لا\".\nوروى صاحب \"المطلع\" عن الفراء، أن العرب إذا ذكرت نكرة ثم أعادتها بنكرة مثلها صارتا اثنتين، كقولك: إذا كسبت درهما فأنفق درهما، فالثاني غير الأول، فإذا أعادتها معرفة فهي هي. وذكر الزجاج نحوه.\nوقال السيد في \"الأمالي\": \"وإنما كان \"العسر\" معرفا و\"اليسر\" منكرا، لأن الاسم إذا تكرر منكرا فالثاني غير الأول، كقولك: جاءني رجل فقلت لرجل: كذا وكذا، وكذلك إن كان الأول معرفة والثاني نكرة، نحو: حضر الرجل، فقلت لرجل: كيت وكيت؛ فإن كان الأول نكرة والثاني معرفة، فالثاني هو الأول، وكذلك ذكر المعرفة بعد المعرفة، نحو: حضر الرجل. فأكرمت الرجل، ولذلك قال ابن عباس: (لن يغلب عسر يسرين) \".","layers":[{"id":"K094.05","heading":"K094.05 — “One hardship will not overcome two eases”","text":"If you ask about the saying of Ibn ʿAbbās and Ibn Masʿūd, “One hardship will not overcome two eases”—also transmitted as a report traced to the Prophet, who came out one day laughing and saying, “One hardship will not overcome two eases”—I answer: this proceeds on the apparent sense and rests on strong hope, and on the principle that God's promise should be understood only in the fullest and most ample sense that its wording can bear.\nThe explanation is that the second sentence may repeat the first, as “woe that day to those who deny” is repeated to establish its meaning in people's minds and fix it in their hearts, or as an individual word is repeated in “Zayd came to me—Zayd.” Alternatively, the first sentence may promise that hardship will necessarily be followed by ease, and the second may be a fresh promise that hardship will be followed by ease. On the interpretation that it is a new statement, there are two eases.\nThe hardship is one because its definiteness must either refer to something already known—the hardship in which they were living—in which case it is the same hardship, just as Zayd is the same in “With Zayd there is wealth; with Zayd there is wealth”; or it refers to the class familiar to everyone, in which case it is likewise the same. Ease, however, is indefinite and denotes some member of its class. If the second statement is new rather than a repetition, it unquestionably denotes a member other than the first.\nIf you ask what the two eases mean, I answer: they may mean the conquests made easy for them in the days of God's Messenger and those made easy in the days of the caliphs; or ease in this world and ease in the next. Compare: “Say: Do you await for us anything but one of the two good outcomes?” (9:52), namely the good of victory and the good of reward.\nIf you ask what the indefiniteness signifies, I answer: magnification—as though He said, “With hardship comes a mighty ease—and what an ease!” In Ibn Masʿūd's codex it occurs only once. If you ask: if it is not repeated in his reading, why did he say, “By Him in whose hand is my soul, if hardship were in a hole, ease would pursue it until it entered upon it; one hardship will not overcome two eases”? I answer: by the two eases he seems to have intended the magnifying force of the indefinite “ease,” interpreting it as the ease of both abodes. Those are indeed two eases.","title":"“One hardship will not overcome two eases”","author":"kashshaf","surah":94,"ar":"فإن قلت: ما معنى قول ابن عباس وابن مسعود رضى الله عنهما: لن يغلب عسر يسرين وقد روى مرفوعا أنه خرج صلى الله عليه وسلم ذات يوم وهو يضحك ويقول «لن يغلب عسر يسرين» ؟ قلت: هذا عمل على الظاهر، وبناء على قوة الرجاء، وأن موعد الله لا يحمل إلا على أو في ما يحتمله اللفظ وأبلغه، والقول في أنه يحتمل أن تكون الجملة الثانية تكريرا للأولى كما كرر قوله فويل يومئذ للمكذبين لتقرير معناها في النفوس وتمكينها في القلوب، وكما يكرر المفرد في قولك: جاءني زيد زيد، وأن تكون الأولى عدة بأن العسر مردوف بيسر لا محالة، والثانية عدة مستأنفة بأن العسر متبوع بيسر، فهما يسران على تقدير الاستئناف، وإنما كان العسر واحدا لأنه لا يخلو، إما أن يكون تعريفه للعهد وهو العسر الذي كانوا فيه، فهو هو، لأن حكمه حكم زيد في قولك:\n\nإن مع زيد مالا، إن مع زيد مالا. وإما أن يكون للجنس الذي يعلمه كل أحد فهو هو أيضا.\n\nوأما اليسر فمنكر متناول لبعض الجنس، فإذا كان الكلام الثاني متأنفا غير مكرر فقد تناول بعضا غير البعض الأول بغير إشكال. فإن قلت: فما المراد باليسرين؟ قلت: يجوز أن يراد بهما \n\nما تيسر لهم من الفتوح في أيام رسول الله صلى الله عليه وسلم وما تيسر لهم في أيام الخلفاء ، وأن يراد يسر الدنيا ويسر الآخرة، كقوله تعالى قل هل تربصون بنا إلا إحدى الحسنيين وهما حسنى الظفر وحسنى الثواب. فإن قلت فما معنى هذا التنكير؟ قلت: التفخيم، كأنه قيل إن مع العسر يسرا عظيما وأى يسر، وهو في مصحف ابن مسعود مرة واحدة. فإن قلت: فإذا ثبت في قراءته غير مكرر، فلم قال: والذي نفسي بيده، لو كان العسر في جحر لطلبه اليسر حتى يدخل عليه، إنه لن يغلب عسر يسرين ؟ قلت: كأنه قصد باليسرين: ما في قوله يسرا من معنى التفخيم، فتأوله بيسر الدارين، وذلك يسران في الحقيقة.","html":"<p>If you ask about the saying of Ibn ʿAbbās and Ibn Masʿūd, “One hardship will not overcome two eases”—also transmitted as a report traced to the Prophet, who came out one day laughing and saying, “One hardship will not overcome two eases”—I answer: this proceeds on the apparent sense and rests on strong hope, and on the principle that God&#x27;s promise should be understood only in the fullest and most ample sense that its wording can bear.\nThe explanation is that the second sentence may repeat the first, as “woe that day to those who deny” is repeated to establish its meaning in people&#x27;s minds and fix it in their hearts, or as an individual word is repeated in “Zayd came to me—Zayd.” Alternatively, the first sentence may promise that hardship will necessarily be followed by ease, and the second may be a fresh promise that hardship will be followed by ease. On the interpretation that it is a new statement, there are two eases.\nThe hardship is one because its definiteness must either refer to something already known—the hardship in which they were living—in which case it is the same hardship, just as Zayd is the same in “With Zayd there is wealth; with Zayd there is wealth”; or it refers to the class familiar to everyone, in which case it is likewise the same. Ease, however, is indefinite and denotes some member of its class. If the second statement is new rather than a repetition, it unquestionably denotes a member other than the first.\nIf you ask what the two eases mean, I answer: they may mean the conquests made easy for them in the days of God&#x27;s Messenger and those made easy in the days of the caliphs; or ease in this world and ease in the next. Compare: “Say: Do you await for us anything but one of the two good outcomes?” (9:52), namely the good of victory and the good of reward.\nIf you ask what the indefiniteness signifies, I answer: magnification—as though He said, “With hardship comes a mighty ease—and what an ease!” In Ibn Masʿūd&#x27;s codex it occurs only once. If you ask: if it is not repeated in his reading, why did he say, “By Him in whose hand is my soul, if hardship were in a hole, ease would pursue it until it entered upon it; one hardship will not overcome two eases”? I answer: by the two eases he seems to have intended the magnifying force of the indefinite “ease,” interpreting it as the ease of both abodes. Those are indeed two eases.</p>","lemma":""},{"id":"T094.08","heading":"T094.08 — The report and the apparent sense (498)","text":"On “it has been transmitted as a report traced to the Prophet”: Mālik transmits in al-Muwaṭṭaʾ from Zayd ibn Aslam: “Abū ʿUbayda wrote to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, mentioning the assembled Roman forces and his apprehension concerning them. ʿUmar wrote back: To proceed: whatever hardship befalls a believing servant, God brings relief after it. One hardship will not overcome two eases.”\nOn “this proceeds on the apparent sense”: ‘apparent’ (ẓāhir) means wording admitting more than one interpretation, with one of them rendered more probable by a cogent indication. What they said follows the apparent sense: the revelation admits both repetition and a new statement. The indication favoring one possibility—the new statement, because it is fuller and more ample—is that God's promise is to be understood in the fullest of the possible senses. “And on the principle that God's promise…” is an explanatory coordination with “and rests on strong hope,” which is similarly related to “proceeds on the apparent sense.” “The explanation is…” sets out the two possibilities.","title":"The report and the apparent sense (498)","author":"tibi","on":["K094.05"],"anchor":"K094.05","surah":94,"ar":"قوله: (وقد روي مرفوعا)، روى مالك في \"الموطأ\" عن زيد بن أسلم، قال: \"كتب أبو عبيدة إلى عمر بن الخطاب رضي الله عنهما، يذكر له جموعا من الروم وما يتخوف منهم، فكتب إليه عمر رضي الله عنه: أما بعد، فإنه مهما ينزل بعبد مؤمن شدة، يجعل الله بعده فرجا، ولن يغلب عسر يسرين\".\n\nقوله: (هذا عمل على الظاهر)، والمعنى بالظاهر: اللفظ المحتمل الراجح أحد محتملاته بقرينة ناهضة، يعني: ما ذكروه عمل بالظاهر؛ فإن ما في التنزيل يحتمل التكرير والاستئناف، والقرينة التي ترجح أحد الاحتمالين، أي: الاستئناف لأنه أوفاهما وأبلغهما، هي أن مبنى \"أن موعد الله لا يحمل إلا على أوفى الاحتمالين\"، عطف تفسيري على قوله: \"وبناء على قوة الرجاء\"، وهو على \"عمل بالظاهر\" كذلك. وقوله: \"والقول فيه\" إلى آخره، بيان للاحتمالين.","html":"<p>On “it has been transmitted as a report traced to the Prophet”: Mālik <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(مَالِك)</bdi> transmits in al-Muwaṭṭaʾ from Zayd ibn Aslam: “Abū ʿUbayda wrote to ʿUmar ibn al-Khaṭṭāb, mentioning the assembled Roman forces and his apprehension concerning them. ʿUmar wrote back: To proceed: whatever hardship befalls a believing servant, God brings relief after it. One hardship will not overcome two eases.”\nOn “this proceeds on the apparent sense”: ‘apparent’ (ẓāhir <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(ظاهر)</bdi>) means wording admitting more than one interpretation, with one of them rendered more probable by a cogent indication. What they said follows the apparent sense: the revelation admits both repetition and a new statement. The indication favoring one possibility—the new statement, because it is fuller and more ample—is that God&#x27;s promise is to be understood in the fullest of the possible senses. “And on the principle that God&#x27;s promise…” is an explanatory coordination with “and rests on strong hope,” which is similarly related to “proceeds on the apparent sense.” “The explanation is…” sets out the two possibilities.</p>","lemma":"وقد روي مرفوعا"},{"id":"T094.09","heading":"T094.09 — How the unrepeated reading can also signify two eases (499)","text":"On this basis, even without repetition—as in Ibn Masʿūd's reading—the intended meaning is conveyed. The indefinite yusran can signify either some instance of ease or magnification. Since the matter rests on strong hope, the latter is preferred. The difference is that the first interpretation signifies the intended meaning by linguistic convention, as will follow, while the second signifies it by implication and indirect expression (kināya). Magnification in yusran requires ease to reach its utmost extent; otherwise it would not mean ease in both abodes. From this follows the multiplicity of ease, and the saying, “One hardship will not overcome two eases.” This is what he indicates by “those are indeed two eases.”\nIf this meaning is intended through repetition, it is more eloquent than treating the sentence as a fresh statement. Without the transmitted saying and prophetic report drawing attention to this subtle point, it would not be understood. One may also say: since the verse concerns the noble Companions and promises them relief after distress, it must be understood as ease in both abodes. In this world that consists of wealth after poverty, strength after weakness, and honor after humiliation. As for the next world, there is no dispute about it.","title":"How the unrepeated reading can also signify two eases (499)","author":"tibi","on":["K094.05"],"anchor":"K094.05","surah":94,"ar":"فعلى هذا، لو لم يكرر - كما هي قراءة ابن مسعود، - أفاد المراد المقصود، وذلك أن التنكير في {يسرا}، يحتمل أن يراد منه بعض من اليسر، وأن يراد منه التفخيم، ولما كان بناء الأمر على قوة الرجاء، رجح الثاني. والفرق بين هذا والأول أن دلالة الأول على المراد بالوضع كما سيجيء، ودلالة الثاني عليه باللزوم والكناية؛ فإن التفخيم في {يسرا}، اقتضى أن يتناهي في، ولو لم يكن متناهيا فيه، إذن لم يرد به يسر الدارين، ولزم من ذلك تعدد اليسر، وأن يقال: \"لن يغلب عسر يسرين\"، وإليه الإشارة بقوله: \"وذلك يسران في الحقيقة\". وإذا ذهب إلى هذا المعني في التكرير، كان أبلغ من الاستئناف، ولولا التنبيه بالأثر والحديث على هذه اللطيفة، لم يفهم ذلك. ويمكن أن يقال: لما كان ورود الآية في حق الصحابة الكرام، ووعدا لهم بالفرج بعد الشدة، أوجب أن يحمل على يسر الدارين: أما في الدنيا، فبالغنى بعد الفقر، والقوة بعد الضعف، وبالعز بعد الذل. وأما في الآخرة، فلا كلام فيه.","html":"<p>On this basis, even without repetition—as in Ibn Masʿūd&#x27;s reading—the intended meaning is conveyed. The indefinite yusran <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(يسرًا)</bdi> can signify either some instance of ease or magnification. Since the matter rests on strong hope, the latter is preferred. The difference is that the first interpretation signifies the intended meaning by linguistic convention, as will follow, while the second signifies it by implication and indirect expression (kināya). Magnification in yusran <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(يسرًا)</bdi> requires ease to reach its utmost extent; otherwise it would not mean ease in both abodes. From this follows the multiplicity of ease, and the saying, “One hardship will not overcome two eases.” This is what he indicates by “those are indeed two eases.”\nIf this meaning is intended through repetition, it is more eloquent than treating the sentence as a fresh statement. Without the transmitted saying and prophetic report drawing attention to this subtle point, it would not be understood. One may also say: since the verse concerns the noble Companions and promises them relief after distress, it must be understood as ease in both abodes. In this world that consists of wealth after poverty, strength after weakness, and honor after humiliation. As for the next world, there is no dispute about it.</p>","lemma":""},{"id":"T094.10","heading":"T094.10 — Definite and indefinite repetition (499–500)","text":"On “the hardship is one”: know that, according to the exact investigators, the definite article lām is assigned to indication and reference to something known. The author of al-Takhmīr says: “The lām is for indication itself. Sometimes the indication points to an individual already familiar to the addressee, and sometimes to a class. Its meaning is one in every case. Understand this: people's errors concerning it are considerable, and it is a point of doctrinal grammatical importance.”\nI say: it consequently requires something previously available to be indicated. Whenever the discourse contains something that can serve as such a referent in any way, that is selected. Al-Bazdawī says: “The defining lām refers back to what is known: a thing is mentioned and then returned to, the second being the first. An example is our scholars' ruling about someone who acknowledges a thousand with a particular qualification, then acknowledges it again with the same qualification: the second is the first. If each expression is indefinite, there is disagreement over whether unity of the sitting is a condition for the second to be identical with the first. According to Abū Ḥanīfa it is; according to Abū Yūsuf it is not.”\nThe author of al-Muṭliʿ transmits from al-Farrāʾ that when the Arabs mention an indefinite noun and then repeat an indefinite noun like it, they become two: “When you earn a dirham, spend a dirham.” The second differs from the first. If they repeat it in definite form, it is the same. Al-Zajjāj states something similar.\nAl-Sayyid says in al-Amālī: “Hardship is definite and ease indefinite because, when a noun is repeated indefinitely, the second differs from the first: ‘A man came to me, and I said to a man such-and-such.’ The same applies when the first is definite and the second indefinite: ‘The man arrived, and I said to a man such-and-such.’ If the first is indefinite and the second definite, the second is the first; likewise when a definite noun follows a definite noun: ‘The man arrived, and I honored the man.’ Hence Ibn ʿAbbās said, ‘One hardship will not overcome two eases.’”","title":"Definite and indefinite repetition (499–500)","author":"tibi","on":["K094.05"],"anchor":"K094.05","surah":94,"ar":"قوله: \"وإنما كان العسر واحدا)، إلى آخره، اعلم أن لام التعريف عند المحقيين موضوعة للإشارة والعهد، قال صاحب \"التخمير\": \"اعلم أن اللام لنفس الإشارة، لكن الإشارة\n\nتقع تارة إلى فرد لمخاطبك به عهد، وأخرى إلى جنس؛ فمعنى اللام واحد على كل حال فاعرفه؛ فإن غلط الناس فيه عظيم، وهي فائدة مذهبية\".\n\nقلت: فإذن لا بد له من تقدم مشار إليه، فإذا جاء في الكلام ما يصلح أن يكون مشارا إليه بأي وجه كان، تعين له، قال البزدوي: \"اللام المعرفة للعهد، وهو أن يذكر شيئا ثم يعاوده، فيكون الثاني هو الأول، مثاله قول علمائنا فيمن أقر بألف مقيدا بقيد، ثم أقر به كذلك أن الثاني هو الأول، وإذا كان كل واحد منهما نكرة، جاء الخلاف في أن اتحاد المجلس شرط لأن يكون الثاني عين الأول، فعند أبي حنيفة رحمه الله: نعم، وعند أبي يوسف: لا\".\n\nوروى صاحب \"المطلع\" عن الفراء، أن العرب إذا ذكرت نكرة ثم أعادتها بنكرة مثلها صارتا اثنتين، كقولك: إذا كسبت درهما فأنفق درهما، فالثاني غير الأول، فإذا أعادتها معرفة فهي هي. وذكر الزجاج نحوه.\n\nوقال السيد في \"الأمالي\": \"وإنما كان \"العسر\" معرفا و\"اليسر\" منكرا، لأن الاسم إذا تكرر منكرا فالثاني غير الأول، كقولك: جاءني رجل فقلت لرجل: كذا وكذا، وكذلك إن كان الأول معرفة والثاني نكرة، نحو: حضر الرجل، فقلت لرجل: كيت وكيت؛ فإن كان الأول نكرة والثاني معرفة، فالثاني هو الأول، وكذلك ذكر المعرفة بعد المعرفة، نحو: حضر الرجل. فأكرمت الرجل، ولذلك قال ابن عباس: (لن يغلب عسر يسرين) \".","html":"<p>On “the hardship is one”: know that, according to the exact investigators, the definite article lām <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(لام (ل))</bdi> is assigned to indication and reference to something known. The author of al-Takhmīr says: “The lām <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(لام (ل))</bdi> is for indication itself. Sometimes the indication points to an individual already familiar to the addressee, and sometimes to a class. Its meaning is one in every case. Understand this: people&#x27;s errors concerning it are considerable, and it is a point of doctrinal grammatical importance.”\nI say: it consequently requires something previously available to be indicated. Whenever the discourse contains something that can serve as such a referent in any way, that is selected. Al-Bazdawī says: “The defining lām <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(لام (ل))</bdi> refers back to what is known: a thing is mentioned and then returned to, the second being the first. An example is our scholars&#x27; ruling about someone who acknowledges a thousand with a particular qualification, then acknowledges it again with the same qualification: the second is the first. If each expression is indefinite, there is disagreement over whether unity of the sitting is a condition for the second to be identical with the first. According to Abū Ḥanīfa it is; according to Abū Yūsuf it is not.”\nThe author of al-Muṭliʿ transmits from al-Farrāʾ that when the Arabs mention an indefinite noun and then repeat an indefinite noun like it, they become two: “When you earn a dirham, spend a dirham.” The second differs from the first. If they repeat it in definite form, it is the same. Al-Zajjāj states something similar.\nAl-Sayyid says in al-Amālī: “Hardship is definite and ease indefinite because, when a noun is repeated indefinitely, the second differs from the first: ‘A man came to me, and I said to a man such-and-such.’ The same applies when the first is definite and the second indefinite: ‘The man arrived, and I said to a man such-and-such.’ If the first is indefinite and the second definite, the second is the first; likewise when a definite noun follows a definite noun: ‘The man arrived, and I honored the man.’ Hence Ibn ʿAbbās said, ‘One hardship will not overcome two eases.’”</p>","lemma":""}],"cairo_notes":[],"verses":[{"key":"94:5","number":5,"ar":"فَإِنَّ مَعَ ٱلْعُسْرِ يُسْرًا","en":"But lo! with hardship goeth ease"},{"key":"94:6","number":6,"ar":"إِنَّ مَعَ ٱلْعُسْرِ يُسْرًا","en":"Lo! with hardship goeth ease"}],"quran_ar":"فَإِنَّ مَعَ ٱلْعُسْرِ يُسْرًا ﴿5﴾ إِنَّ مَعَ ٱلْعُسْرِ يُسْرًا ﴿6﴾","quran_en":"5. But lo! with hardship goeth ease 6. Lo! with hardship goeth ease"},{"id":"K094.06","q":"94:7–8","s":94,"surah":"al-Sharḥ","surah_ar":"الشرح","title":"al-Sharḥ · Verses 7–8: continuing in worship","cairo":"K IV:770–772 · T XVI:492–503","source_page_first":770,"source_page_last":772,"chunk":"passages-094","segments":[{"type":"exposition","title":"Verses 7–8: continuing in worship","html":"<p>“So when you have finished, exert yourself; and direct your longing toward your Lord.”\nIf you ask how “so when you have finished, exert yourself” connects with what precedes, I answer: after enumerating His earlier blessings and promising those to come, He urged him to gratitude, diligence in worship, and exertion in it: to join one act of worship to another, pursue them successively, and take care that none of his times be empty of them. Whenever he finishes one act of worship, he follows it with another.\nIbn ʿAbbās said: when you finish your prayer, exert yourself in supplication. Al-Ḥasan said: when you finish warfare, exert yourself in worship. Mujāhid said: when you finish your worldly business, exert yourself in prayer. Al-Shaʿbī saw a man lifting a stone and said: “This is not what someone with time to spare has been commanded to do.” Sitting idle without occupation, or occupying oneself with what benefits neither one&#x27;s religion nor one&#x27;s worldly affairs, is evidence of foolish judgment, feeble understanding, and heedlessness taking control. ʿUmar said: “I dislike seeing any of you idle and aimless, engaged neither in work for this world nor in work for the next.”\nAbū al-Sammāl read fariġta, with kasra <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(كَسْرَة (ـِ))</bdi> on the rāʾ <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(راء (ر))</bdi>; this is not an eloquent usage. Among novel interpretations is the report that one of the Rāfiḍa read fa-nṣib <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(فانْصِب)</bdi>, with kasra <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(كَسْرَة (ـِ))</bdi> on the ṣād <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(صاد (ص))</bdi>, meaning: appoint ʿAlī to the imamate. Were this valid for the Rāfiḍī, it would equally be valid for a Nāṣibī to read it in this fashion and make it a command to naṣb <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(نصب)</bdi>, which is hatred and hostility toward ʿAlī.\n“And direct your longing toward your Lord”: make Him exclusively the object of your longing; ask only for His bounty, relying upon Him. Fa-raġġib is also read: encourage people to seek what is with Him.\nThe Prophet is reported to have said: “Whoever recites ‘Have We not expanded,’ it is as though he came to me while I was grieving and relieved my grief.”</p>","author":"kashshaf","source_id":"K094.06"},{"type":"exposition","title":"The question about indefiniteness; successive acts of devotion (501–502)","html":"<p>On “then what does this indefiniteness mean?”: the fāʾ <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(فاء (ف))</bdi> indicates an objection. If the two eases mean what you have explained in either of the two ways, they ought to be definite; why, then, are they indefinite?\nOn “when you finish your prayer, exert yourself in supplication”: this is coordinated with “when he finishes one act of worship, he follows it with another.” Both “you have finished” and “exert yourself” are unrestricted. They may remain unrestricted: when you finish one act of worship, follow it with another. Or they may be specified as prayer and supplication, because prayer is the best of the acts of worship and supplication is its marrow; or as warfare and worship, as in the saying, “We have returned from the lesser struggle to the greater struggle”; or as worldly business and prayer, because ‘having free time’ is most often used of worldly affairs. An example is the report, “[Take advantage of] your free time before you become occupied.” This transmission from Mujāhid is mentioned in Sharḥ <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(شرح)</bdi> al-Sunna.\nOn “idle and aimless” (fāriġan <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(فارغًا)</bdi> sabahlalan <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(سبهللًا)</bdi>): al-Nihāya says: “In ʿUmar&#x27;s report, ‘I dislike seeing any of you aimless, engaged neither in work for this world nor in work for the next,’ the indefiniteness in ‘world’ and ‘next world’ goes back to the noun annexed to them, namely ‘work’: neither in any work belonging to this world&#x27;s affairs nor in any work belonging to the next world&#x27;s affairs. One says jāʾa yamshī <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(جاء يمشي)</bdi> sabahlalan <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(سبهللًا)</bdi> when someone comes and goes idly, engaged in nothing.”</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T094.11"},{"type":"exposition","title":"Exclusive longing and the unusual conjunction (503)","html":"<p>On “make Him exclusively the object of your longing”: particularization is conveyed by placing the prepositional phrase before the verb. Al-Sayyid says in al-Amālī: “The fāʾ <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(فاء (ف))</bdi> occurs together with the wāw <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(واو (و))</bdi>, and ilā <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(إلى)</bdi> is governed by what comes after the fāʾ <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(فاء (ف))</bdi>. Similarly, in ‘and your garments—purify them’ (74:4), what precedes the fāʾ <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(فاء (ف))</bdi> is made accusative by what follows it. This is among the remarkable features of their speech: ordinarily fāʾ <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(فاء (ف))</bdi> coordinates, introduces a response, or introduces something resembling a response, such as the predicate of an incomplete noun—that is, a relative pronoun whose relative clause is verbal. Here it falls outside its assigned function.”\nThe sūrah is complete, by God&#x27;s praise and aid. God suffices us; how excellent a trustee He is.</p>","author":"tibi","source_id":"T094.12"}],"types":["exposition","language","theology","reports"],"ar":"فإذا فرغت فانصب (7) وإلى ربك فارغب (8)\nفإن قلت: فكيف تعلق قوله فإذا فرغت فانصب بما قبله؟ قلت: لما عدد عليه نعمه السالفة ووعده الآنفة، بعثه على الشكر والاجتهاد في العبادة والنصب فيها، وأن يواصل بين بعضها وبعض، ويتابع ويحرص على أن لا يخلى وقتا من أوقاته منها. فإذا فرغ من عبادة ذنبها بأخرى. وعن ابن عباس: فإذا فرغت من صلاتك فاجتهد في الدعاء. وعن الحسن: فإذا فرغت من الغزو فاجتهد في العبادة. وعن مجاهد: فإذا فرغت من دنياك فانصب في صلاتك. وعن الشعبي: أنه رأى رجلا يشيل حجرا فقال: ليس بهذا أمر الفارغ، وقعود الرجل فارغا من غير شغل أو اشتغاله بما لا يعنيه في دينه أو دنياه: من سفه الرأى وسخافة العقل واستيلاء الغفلة، ولقد قال عمر رضى الله عنه: إنى لأكره أن أرى أحدكم فارغا سهلا لا في عمل دنيا ولا في عمل آخرة . وقرأ أبو السمال: فرغت- بكسر الراء- وليست بفصيحة. ومن البدع: ما روى عن بعض الرافضة أنه قرأ فانصب بكسر الصاد، أى فانصب عليا للإمامة، ولو صح هذا للرافضى لصح الناصبي أن يقرأ هكذا، ويجعله أمرا بالنصب الذي هو بغض على وعداوته وإلى ربك فارغب واجعل رغبتك إليه خصوصا، ولا تسأل إلا فضله متوكلا عليه. وقرئ: فرغب أى: رغب الناس إلى طلب ما عنده.\nعن النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم: «من قرأ ألم نشرح، فكأنما جاءني وأنا مغتم ففرج عنى»\nقوله: (فما معنى هذا التنكير؟ )، دل الفاء على إنكار، يعني: إذا أريد باليسرين ما ذكرت من الوجهين، فالواجب أن يجاء بهما معرفتين، فما معنى التنكير؟\nقوله: (فإذا فرغت من صلاتك فاجتهد في الدعاء)، عطف على قوله: \"فإذا فرغ من عبادة ذنبها بأخرى\"، فقوله {فرغت فانصب} كلاهما مطلقان؛ يجوز أن يجريا على إطلاقهما بأن\nيقال: فإذا فرغت من عبادة ذنبها بأخرى. وأن يخصصا بالصلاة والدعاء لأن الصلاة أفضل العبادات والدعاء مخها، أو بالغزو والعبادة كما قيل: \"رجعنا من الجهاد الأصغر إلى الجهاد الأكبر\"، أو بالدنيا والصلاة، لأن الفراغ أكثر ما يستعمل في الأمور الدنيوية، ومنه الحديث: \"فراغك قبل شغلك\"، وهذه الرواية مذكورة في \"شرح السنة\" عن مجاهد.\nقوله: (فارغا سبهللا)، النهاية: \"في حديث عمر رضي الله عنه: \"إني لأكره أن أرى أحدكم سبهللا، لا في عمل دنيا ولا في عمل آخرة\". التنكير في \"دنيا\" و\"آخرة\" يرجع إلى المضاف إليهما، وهو العمل، كأنه قال: لا في عمل من أعمال الدنيا، ولا في عمل من أعمال الآخرة. يقال: جاء يمشي سبهللا، إذا جاء وذهب فارغا في غير شيء\".\nقوله: (واجعل رغبتك إليع خصوصا)، التخصيص يفيده تقديم الجار والمجرور على الفعل، قال السيد في \"الأمالي\": \"جامعت الفاء والواو، \"وإلى\" متعلقة بما بعد الفاء. ومثله {وثيابك فطهر} [المدثر: 4]؛ انتصب ما قبل الفاء بما بعدها، وهذا من عجيب كلامهم؛ لأن الفاء تعطف أو تدخل في الجواب وما أشبه الجواب، كخبر الاسم الناقص، أي الموصولة التي صلتها الفعل، وهي هاهنا خارجة عما وضعت له\".\nتمت السورة\nبحمد الله وعونه\nوحسبنا الله ونعم الوكيل","layers":[{"id":"K094.06","heading":"K094.06 — Verses 7–8: continuing in worship","text":"“So when you have finished, exert yourself; and direct your longing toward your Lord.”\nIf you ask how “so when you have finished, exert yourself” connects with what precedes, I answer: after enumerating His earlier blessings and promising those to come, He urged him to gratitude, diligence in worship, and exertion in it: to join one act of worship to another, pursue them successively, and take care that none of his times be empty of them. Whenever he finishes one act of worship, he follows it with another.\nIbn ʿAbbās said: when you finish your prayer, exert yourself in supplication. Al-Ḥasan said: when you finish warfare, exert yourself in worship. Mujāhid said: when you finish your worldly business, exert yourself in prayer. Al-Shaʿbī saw a man lifting a stone and said: “This is not what someone with time to spare has been commanded to do.” Sitting idle without occupation, or occupying oneself with what benefits neither one's religion nor one's worldly affairs, is evidence of foolish judgment, feeble understanding, and heedlessness taking control. ʿUmar said: “I dislike seeing any of you idle and aimless, engaged neither in work for this world nor in work for the next.”\nAbū al-Sammāl read fariġta, with kasra on the rāʾ; this is not an eloquent usage. Among novel interpretations is the report that one of the Rāfiḍa read fa-nṣib, with kasra on the ṣād, meaning: appoint ʿAlī to the imamate. Were this valid for the Rāfiḍī, it would equally be valid for a Nāṣibī to read it in this fashion and make it a command to naṣb, which is hatred and hostility toward ʿAlī.\n“And direct your longing toward your Lord”: make Him exclusively the object of your longing; ask only for His bounty, relying upon Him. Fa-raġġib is also read: encourage people to seek what is with Him.\nThe Prophet is reported to have said: “Whoever recites ‘Have We not expanded,’ it is as though he came to me while I was grieving and relieved my grief.”","title":"Verses 7–8: continuing in worship","author":"kashshaf","surah":94,"ar":"فإذا فرغت فانصب (7) وإلى ربك فارغب (8)\n\nفإن قلت: فكيف تعلق قوله فإذا فرغت فانصب بما قبله؟ قلت: لما عدد عليه نعمه السالفة ووعده الآنفة، بعثه على الشكر والاجتهاد في العبادة والنصب فيها، وأن يواصل بين بعضها وبعض، ويتابع ويحرص على أن لا يخلى وقتا من أوقاته منها. فإذا فرغ من عبادة ذنبها بأخرى. وعن ابن عباس: فإذا فرغت من صلاتك فاجتهد في الدعاء. وعن الحسن: فإذا فرغت من الغزو فاجتهد في العبادة. وعن مجاهد: فإذا فرغت من دنياك فانصب في صلاتك. وعن الشعبي: أنه رأى رجلا يشيل حجرا فقال: ليس بهذا أمر الفارغ، وقعود الرجل فارغا من غير شغل أو اشتغاله بما لا يعنيه في دينه أو دنياه: من سفه الرأى وسخافة العقل واستيلاء الغفلة، ولقد قال عمر رضى الله عنه: إنى لأكره أن أرى أحدكم فارغا سهلا لا في عمل دنيا ولا في عمل آخرة . وقرأ أبو السمال: فرغت- بكسر الراء- وليست بفصيحة. ومن البدع: ما روى عن بعض الرافضة أنه قرأ فانصب بكسر الصاد، أى فانصب عليا للإمامة، ولو صح هذا للرافضى لصح الناصبي أن يقرأ هكذا، ويجعله أمرا بالنصب الذي هو بغض على وعداوته وإلى ربك فارغب واجعل رغبتك إليه خصوصا، ولا تسأل إلا فضله متوكلا عليه. وقرئ: فرغب أى: رغب الناس إلى طلب ما عنده.\n\nعن النبي صلى الله عليه وسلم: «من قرأ ألم نشرح، فكأنما جاءني وأنا مغتم ففرج عنى»","html":"<p>“So when you have finished, exert yourself; and direct your longing toward your Lord.”\nIf you ask how “so when you have finished, exert yourself” connects with what precedes, I answer: after enumerating His earlier blessings and promising those to come, He urged him to gratitude, diligence in worship, and exertion in it: to join one act of worship to another, pursue them successively, and take care that none of his times be empty of them. Whenever he finishes one act of worship, he follows it with another.\nIbn ʿAbbās said: when you finish your prayer, exert yourself in supplication. Al-Ḥasan said: when you finish warfare, exert yourself in worship. Mujāhid said: when you finish your worldly business, exert yourself in prayer. Al-Shaʿbī saw a man lifting a stone and said: “This is not what someone with time to spare has been commanded to do.” Sitting idle without occupation, or occupying oneself with what benefits neither one&#x27;s religion nor one&#x27;s worldly affairs, is evidence of foolish judgment, feeble understanding, and heedlessness taking control. ʿUmar said: “I dislike seeing any of you idle and aimless, engaged neither in work for this world nor in work for the next.”\nAbū al-Sammāl read fariġta, with kasra <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(كَسْرَة (ـِ))</bdi> on the rāʾ <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(راء (ر))</bdi>; this is not an eloquent usage. Among novel interpretations is the report that one of the Rāfiḍa read fa-nṣib <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(فانْصِب)</bdi>, with kasra <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(كَسْرَة (ـِ))</bdi> on the ṣād <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(صاد (ص))</bdi>, meaning: appoint ʿAlī to the imamate. Were this valid for the Rāfiḍī, it would equally be valid for a Nāṣibī to read it in this fashion and make it a command to naṣb <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(نصب)</bdi>, which is hatred and hostility toward ʿAlī.\n“And direct your longing toward your Lord”: make Him exclusively the object of your longing; ask only for His bounty, relying upon Him. Fa-raġġib is also read: encourage people to seek what is with Him.\nThe Prophet is reported to have said: “Whoever recites ‘Have We not expanded,’ it is as though he came to me while I was grieving and relieved my grief.”</p>","lemma":""},{"id":"T094.11","heading":"T094.11 — The question about indefiniteness; successive acts of devotion (501–502)","text":"On “then what does this indefiniteness mean?”: the fāʾ indicates an objection. If the two eases mean what you have explained in either of the two ways, they ought to be definite; why, then, are they indefinite?\nOn “when you finish your prayer, exert yourself in supplication”: this is coordinated with “when he finishes one act of worship, he follows it with another.” Both “you have finished” and “exert yourself” are unrestricted. They may remain unrestricted: when you finish one act of worship, follow it with another. Or they may be specified as prayer and supplication, because prayer is the best of the acts of worship and supplication is its marrow; or as warfare and worship, as in the saying, “We have returned from the lesser struggle to the greater struggle”; or as worldly business and prayer, because ‘having free time’ is most often used of worldly affairs. An example is the report, “[Take advantage of] your free time before you become occupied.” This transmission from Mujāhid is mentioned in Sharḥ al-Sunna.\nOn “idle and aimless” (fāriġan sabahlalan): al-Nihāya says: “In ʿUmar's report, ‘I dislike seeing any of you aimless, engaged neither in work for this world nor in work for the next,’ the indefiniteness in ‘world’ and ‘next world’ goes back to the noun annexed to them, namely ‘work’: neither in any work belonging to this world's affairs nor in any work belonging to the next world's affairs. One says jāʾa yamshī sabahlalan when someone comes and goes idly, engaged in nothing.”","title":"The question about indefiniteness; successive acts of devotion (501–502)","author":"tibi","on":["K094.05","K094.06"],"anchor":"K094.06","surah":94,"ar":"قوله: (فما معنى هذا التنكير؟ )، دل الفاء على إنكار، يعني: إذا أريد باليسرين ما ذكرت من الوجهين، فالواجب أن يجاء بهما معرفتين، فما معنى التنكير؟\n\nقوله: (فإذا فرغت من صلاتك فاجتهد في الدعاء)، عطف على قوله: \"فإذا فرغ من عبادة ذنبها بأخرى\"، فقوله {فرغت فانصب} كلاهما مطلقان؛ يجوز أن يجريا على إطلاقهما بأن\n\nيقال: فإذا فرغت من عبادة ذنبها بأخرى. وأن يخصصا بالصلاة والدعاء لأن الصلاة أفضل العبادات والدعاء مخها، أو بالغزو والعبادة كما قيل: \"رجعنا من الجهاد الأصغر إلى الجهاد الأكبر\"، أو بالدنيا والصلاة، لأن الفراغ أكثر ما يستعمل في الأمور الدنيوية، ومنه الحديث: \"فراغك قبل شغلك\"، وهذه الرواية مذكورة في \"شرح السنة\" عن مجاهد.\n\nقوله: (فارغا سبهللا)، النهاية: \"في حديث عمر رضي الله عنه: \"إني لأكره أن أرى أحدكم سبهللا، لا في عمل دنيا ولا في عمل آخرة\". التنكير في \"دنيا\" و\"آخرة\" يرجع إلى المضاف إليهما، وهو العمل، كأنه قال: لا في عمل من أعمال الدنيا، ولا في عمل من أعمال الآخرة. يقال: جاء يمشي سبهللا، إذا جاء وذهب فارغا في غير شيء\".","html":"<p>On “then what does this indefiniteness mean?”: the fāʾ <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(فاء (ف))</bdi> indicates an objection. If the two eases mean what you have explained in either of the two ways, they ought to be definite; why, then, are they indefinite?\nOn “when you finish your prayer, exert yourself in supplication”: this is coordinated with “when he finishes one act of worship, he follows it with another.” Both “you have finished” and “exert yourself” are unrestricted. They may remain unrestricted: when you finish one act of worship, follow it with another. Or they may be specified as prayer and supplication, because prayer is the best of the acts of worship and supplication is its marrow; or as warfare and worship, as in the saying, “We have returned from the lesser struggle to the greater struggle”; or as worldly business and prayer, because ‘having free time’ is most often used of worldly affairs. An example is the report, “[Take advantage of] your free time before you become occupied.” This transmission from Mujāhid is mentioned in Sharḥ <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(شرح)</bdi> al-Sunna.\nOn “idle and aimless” (fāriġan <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(فارغًا)</bdi> sabahlalan <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(سبهللًا)</bdi>): al-Nihāya says: “In ʿUmar&#x27;s report, ‘I dislike seeing any of you aimless, engaged neither in work for this world nor in work for the next,’ the indefiniteness in ‘world’ and ‘next world’ goes back to the noun annexed to them, namely ‘work’: neither in any work belonging to this world&#x27;s affairs nor in any work belonging to the next world&#x27;s affairs. One says jāʾa yamshī <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(جاء يمشي)</bdi> sabahlalan <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(سبهللًا)</bdi> when someone comes and goes idly, engaged in nothing.”</p>","lemma":"فما معنى هذا التنكير؟ "},{"id":"T094.12","heading":"T094.12 — Exclusive longing and the unusual conjunction (503)","text":"On “make Him exclusively the object of your longing”: particularization is conveyed by placing the prepositional phrase before the verb. Al-Sayyid says in al-Amālī: “The fāʾ occurs together with the wāw, and ilā is governed by what comes after the fāʾ. Similarly, in ‘and your garments—purify them’ (74:4), what precedes the fāʾ is made accusative by what follows it. This is among the remarkable features of their speech: ordinarily fāʾ coordinates, introduces a response, or introduces something resembling a response, such as the predicate of an incomplete noun—that is, a relative pronoun whose relative clause is verbal. Here it falls outside its assigned function.”\nThe sūrah is complete, by God's praise and aid. God suffices us; how excellent a trustee He is.","title":"Exclusive longing and the unusual conjunction (503)","author":"tibi","on":["K094.06"],"anchor":"K094.06","surah":94,"ar":"قوله: (واجعل رغبتك إليع خصوصا)، التخصيص يفيده تقديم الجار والمجرور على الفعل، قال السيد في \"الأمالي\": \"جامعت الفاء والواو، \"وإلى\" متعلقة بما بعد الفاء. ومثله {وثيابك فطهر} [المدثر: 4]؛ انتصب ما قبل الفاء بما بعدها، وهذا من عجيب كلامهم؛ لأن الفاء تعطف أو تدخل في الجواب وما أشبه الجواب، كخبر الاسم الناقص، أي الموصولة التي صلتها الفعل، وهي هاهنا خارجة عما وضعت له\".\n\nتمت السورة\n\nبحمد الله وعونه\n\nوحسبنا الله ونعم الوكيل","html":"<p>On “make Him exclusively the object of your longing”: particularization is conveyed by placing the prepositional phrase before the verb. Al-Sayyid says in al-Amālī: “The fāʾ <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(فاء (ف))</bdi> occurs together with the wāw <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(واو (و))</bdi>, and ilā <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(إلى)</bdi> is governed by what comes after the fāʾ <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(فاء (ف))</bdi>. Similarly, in ‘and your garments—purify them’ (74:4), what precedes the fāʾ <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(فاء (ف))</bdi> is made accusative by what follows it. This is among the remarkable features of their speech: ordinarily fāʾ <bdi class=\"ar-term\" lang=\"ar\" dir=\"rtl\">(فاء (ف))</bdi> coordinates, introduces a response, or introduces something resembling a response, such as the predicate of an incomplete noun—that is, a relative pronoun whose relative clause is verbal. Here it falls outside its assigned function.”\nThe sūrah is complete, by God&#x27;s praise and aid. God suffices us; how excellent a trustee He is.</p>","lemma":"واجعل رغبتك إليع خصوصا"}],"cairo_notes":[],"verses":[{"key":"94:7","number":7,"ar":"فَإِذَا فَرَغْتَ فَٱنصَبْ","en":"So when thou art relieved, still toil"},{"key":"94:8","number":8,"ar":"وَإِلَىٰ رَبِّكَ فَٱرْغَب","en":"And strive to please thy Lord"}],"quran_ar":"فَإِذَا فَرَغْتَ فَٱنصَبْ ﴿7﴾ وَإِلَىٰ رَبِّكَ فَٱرْغَب ﴿8﴾","quran_en":"7. So when thou art relieved, still toil 8. And strive to please thy Lord"}];
